{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06492","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06492","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06492","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kensuke","familyName":"Nakamura","name":"Nakamura, Kensuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Bajcsy","name":"Bajcsy, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Well Do Latent World Models Understand Partially Observable Safety Constraints?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T22:09:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:11:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-06T02:12:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-09T00:19:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-12T09:34:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-14T02:14:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Latent world models are a promising approach for learning state representations and dynamics directly from high-dimensional observations, enabling robot control in hard-to-model settings. However, control performance ultimately depends on the latent representation encoding the required information for the task. In this work, we study latent-space safe control problems and show how partial observability can induce control failures when safety-relevant information is not preserved in the latent state. Specifically, we identify two world model failure modes: estimation gaps, where current observations do not reveal safety-critical quantities (e.g., temperature in a cooking task), and prediction gaps, where failures are observable once they occur but cannot be reliably anticipated from available observations. We introduce two diagnostics for these gaps: a mutual-information-based measure of safety observability and a rollout-based measure of future safety predictability. Finally, we present mitigation strategies for each failure mode: privileged multimodal supervision for estimation gaps and conformal risk calibration for prediction gaps. Across two hardware case studies -- using unimodal RGB world models and multimodal RGB+Tactile and RGB+Thermal variants -- we show that these mitigation strategies improve the safety of a Franka Research 3 manipulator on challenging cooking tasks under partial observability, albeit with increased conservativeness. More broadly, our work raises the question of when world model state representations are sufficient for reliable robot control"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 tables 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06492","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:47:32Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:47:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:20:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06710","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06710","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06710","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongzhi","familyName":"Zang","name":"Zang, Hongzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingjie","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Mingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Si","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Si","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongji","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yongji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peihong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangzhi","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Liangzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhexuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhexuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quanlu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Quanlu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weinan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RLinf-VLA: A Unified and Efficient Framework for Reinforcement Learning of Vision-Language-Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:05:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:29:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-07T10:46:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T01:33:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T08:36:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-13T00:32:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent studies have demonstrated the potential of reinforcement learning (RL) to improve the task performance of vision-language-action (VLA) models through interaction. However, current efforts remain fragmented, lacking a unified platform for fair comparison across architectures and algorithms, as well as an efficient system design for scalable training. Therefore, we present RLinf-VLA, a unified and efficient framework for scalable RL training of VLA models. RLinf-VLA standardizes the integration of diverse VLA architectures, RL algorithms, and heterogeneous simulators through a unified interface, enabling extensibility and reproducibility. To improve efficiency, the framework adopts a flexible resource allocation architecture for rendering, inference, and training in RL pipelines. In particular, RLinf-VLA introduces a hybrid fine-grained pipeline allocation strategy that achieves a 1.61$\\times$-1.88$\\times$ training speedup on ManiSkill. Using this framework, RL-trained models achieve strong performance across embodied benchmarks, including 98.11% success on 130 LIBERO tasks, 97.66% success on 25 ManiSkill tasks, and 84.63% average success across 6 RoboTwin tasks. In addition, RLinf-VLA distills a set of effective practices for RL-based VLA training. We envision RLinf-VLA as a foundational framework for efficient, unified, and reproducible research in embodied intelligence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to RSS 2026. This is the technical report of the RLinf Team, focusing on the algorithm side. For the system-level design, please refer to arXiv:2509.15965. The open-sourced code link: https://github.com/RLinf/RLinf"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06710","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:52:39Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:52:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-13T02:11:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06473","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06473","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06473","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yatao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yatao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konrad","familyName":"Schindler","name":"Schindler, Konrad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Raubal","name":"Raubal, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Deep Generative Model for Human Mobility Behavior"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T21:22:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:09:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-07T20:12:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T01:46:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-08T18:38:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-10T00:06:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-09T17:00:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:12:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding and modeling human mobility is central to challenges in transport planning, sustainable urban design, and public health. Despite decades of effort, simulating individual mobility remains challenging because of its complex, context-dependent, and exploratory nature. Here, building on the activity-based view of daily mobility, we propose MobilityGen, a diffusion-based generative framework for simulating multi-attribute activity-travel sequences over days to weeks at large spatial scales. By linking behavioral attributes with environmental context, MobilityGen reproduces key patterns such as scaling laws for location visits, activity time allocation, and the coupled evolution of travel mode and destination choices. It reflects spatio-temporal variability and generates diverse and plausible mobility patterns consistent with the built environment. Beyond standard validation, MobilityGen enables analyses that have been difficult with earlier models, including how access to urban space varies across travel modes and how co-presence dynamics shape social exposure and segregation. Together, these results support an integrated, data-driven basis for fine-grained studies of human mobility behavior and its societal implications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at TRC"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06473","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:47:06Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:47:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:12:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06299","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06299","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06299","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiago","familyName":"de Conto","name":"de Conto, Tiago","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Armston","name":"Armston, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ralph","familyName":"Dubayah","name":"Dubayah, Ralph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scalable deep fusion of spaceborne lidar and synthetic aperture radar for global forest structural complexity mapping"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:29:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-31T01:00:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1088/3049-4753/ae2c01","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Forest structural complexity metrics integrate multiple canopy attributes into a single value that reflects habitat quality and ecosystem function. Spaceborne lidar from the Global Ecosystem Dynamics Investigation (GEDI) has enabled mapping of structural complexity in temperate and tropical forests, but its sparse sampling limits continuous high-resolution mapping. We present a scalable, deep learning framework fusing GEDI observations with multimodal Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) datasets to produce global, high-resolution (25 m) wall-to-wall maps of forest structural complexity. Our adapted EfficientNetV2 architecture, trained on over 130 million GEDI footprints, achieves high performance (global R2 = 0.82) with fewer than 400,000 parameters, making it an accessible tool that enables researchers to process datasets at any scale without requiring specialized computing infrastructure. The model produces accurate predictions with calibrated uncertainty estimates across biomes and time periods, preserving fine-scale spatial patterns. It has been used to generate a global, multi-temporal dataset of forest structural complexity from 2015 to 2022. Through transfer learning, this framework can be extended to predict additional forest structural variables with minimal computational cost. This approach supports continuous, multi-temporal monitoring of global forest structural dynamics and provides tools for biodiversity conservation and ecosystem management efforts in a changing climate."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06299","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:43:03Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:43:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-31T02:35:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07133","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07133","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07133","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Burak","familyName":"Kantarci","name":"Kantarci, Burak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umair","familyName":"Siddique","name":"Siddique, Umair","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Digital Twin Framework for Metamorphic Testing of Autonomous Driving Systems Using Generative Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:27:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-09T00:50:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/gcwkshps68340.2025.11591078","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ensuring the safety of self-driving cars remains a major challenge due to the complexity and unpredictability of real-world driving environments. Traditional testing methods face significant limitations, such as the oracle problem, which makes it difficult to determine whether a system's behavior is correct, and the inability to cover the full range of scenarios an autonomous vehicle may encounter. In this paper, we introduce a digital twin-driven metamorphic testing framework that addresses these challenges by creating a virtual replica of the self-driving system and its operating environment. By combining digital twin technology with AI-based image generative models such as Stable Diffusion, our approach enables the systematic generation of realistic and diverse driving scenes. This includes variations in weather, road topology, and environmental features, all while maintaining the core semantics of the original scenario. The digital twin provides a synchronized simulation environment where changes can be tested in a controlled and repeatable manner. Within this environment, we define three metamorphic relations inspired by real-world traffic rules and vehicle behavior. We validate our framework in the Udacity self-driving simulator and demonstrate that it significantly enhances test coverage and effectiveness. Our method achieves the highest true positive rate (0.719), F1 score (0.689), and precision (0.662) compared to baseline approaches. This paper highlights the value of integrating digital twins with AI-powered scenario generation to create a scalable, automated, and high-fidelity testing solution for autonomous vehicle safety."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07133","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:39Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:02:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-09T02:02:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06596","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06596","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06596","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayush","familyName":"Zenith","name":"Zenith, Ayush","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnold","familyName":"Zumbrun","name":"Zumbrun, Arnold","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neel","familyName":"Raut","name":"Raut, Neel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SDQM: Synthetic Data Quality Metric for Object Detection Dataset Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.4.8; I.5.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T03:01:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:20:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-10T01:28:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-11T00:19:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1117/1.jei.35.3.033014","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The performance of machine learning models depends heavily on training data. The scarcity of large-scale, well-annotated datasets poses significant challenges in creating robust models. To address this, synthetic data generated through simulations and generative models has emerged as a promising solution, enhancing dataset diversity and improving the performance, reliability, and resilience of models. However, evaluating the quality of this generated data requires an effective metric. We introduce the Synthetic Dataset Quality Metric (SDQM) to assess data quality for object detection tasks without requiring model training to converge. This metric enables more efficient generation and selection of synthetic datasets, addressing a key challenge in resource-constrained object detection tasks. In our experiments, SDQM demonstrated a strong correlation with the mean average precision (mAP) scores of YOLO11, a leading object detection model, whereas previous metrics only exhibited moderate or weak correlations. In addition, it provides actionable insights into improving dataset quality, minimizing the need for costly iterative training. This scalable and efficient metric sets a new standard for evaluating synthetic data. The code for SDQM is available at https://github.com/ayushzenith/SDQM"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted and Published at SPIE: Journal of Electronic Imaging, Vol. 35, Issue 3"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06596","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:49:56Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:49:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-11T02:25:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07135","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07135","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07135","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karim El","familyName":"Khoury","name":"Khoury, Karim El","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maxime","familyName":"Zanella","name":"Zanella, Maxime","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christophe","familyName":"De Vleeschouwer","name":"De Vleeschouwer, Christophe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benoit","familyName":"Macq","name":"Macq, Benoit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Few-Shot Adaptation Benchmark for Remote Sensing Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:29:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-01T01:05:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Remote Sensing Vision-Language Models (RSVLMs) have shown remarkable potential thanks to large-scale pretraining, achieving strong zero-shot performance on various tasks. However, their ability to generalize in low-data regimes, such as few-shot learning, remains insufficiently explored. In this work, we present the first structured benchmark for evaluating few-shot adaptation methods on RSVLMs. We conduct comprehensive experiments across ten remote sensing scene classification datasets, applying five widely used few-shot adaptation strategies to three state-of-the-art RSVLMs with varying backbones. Our findings reveal that models with similar zero-shot performance can exhibit markedly different behavior under few-shot adaptation, with some RSVLMs being inherently more amenable to such adaptation than others. The variability of performance and the absence of a clear winner among existing methods highlight the need for the development of more robust methods for few-shot adaptation tailored to RS. To facilitate future research, we provide a reproducible benchmarking framework and open-source code to systematically evaluate RSVLMs under few-shot conditions. The source code is publicly available on Github: https://github.com/elkhouryk/fewshot_RSVLMs"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07135","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:42Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:02:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-01T02:33:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06843","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06843","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06843","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuhang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zhifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deyi","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Deyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lanyun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Lanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-signals Driven Multi-LLM Debate for Efficient and Accurate Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:10:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:38:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-26T13:10:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:58:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have exhibited impressive capabilities across diverse application domains. Recent work has explored Multi-LLM Agent Debate (MAD) as a way to enhance performance by enabling multiple LLMs to discuss and refine responses iteratively. Nevertheless, existing MAD methods predominantly focus on utilizing external structures, such as debate graphs, using LLM-as-a-Judge, while neglecting the application of self signals, such as token logits and attention, that arise during generation. This omission leads to redundant computation and potential performance degradation. In this paper, we shift the focus to the self signals of multi-LLM debate and introduce a Self-Signals Driven Multi-LLM Debate (SID), which leverages two types of self-signals: model-level confidence and token-level semantic focus, to adaptively guide the debate process. Our approach enables high-confidence agents to exit early at the model level and compress the redundant debate contents based on the attention mechanism. We evaluate our method on various LLMs and Multimodal LLMs across multiple challenging benchmarks. Experimental results demonstrate that our method not only outperforms existing MAD techniques in accuracy but also reduces token consumption, highlighting the effectiveness of utilizing self signals in enhancing both the performance and efficiency of multi-agent debate systems. Our code will be available at~\\href{https://github.com/xuhang2019/SID}{\\texttt{https://github.com/xuhang2019/SID}}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06843","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:45Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:36:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Firoj","familyName":"Alam","name":"Alam, Firoj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali Ezzat","familyName":"Shahroor","name":"Shahroor, Ali Ezzat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md. Arid","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Md. Arid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zien Sheikh","familyName":"Ali","name":"Ali, Zien Sheikh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hunzalah Hassan","familyName":"Bhatti","name":"Bhatti, Hunzalah Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed Bayan","familyName":"Kmainasi","name":"Kmainasi, Mohamed Bayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shammur Absar","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Shammur Absar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Basel","familyName":"Mousi","name":"Mousi, Basel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fahim","familyName":"Dalvi","name":"Dalvi, Fahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nadir","familyName":"Durrani","name":"Durrani, Nadir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natasa","familyName":"Milic-Frayling","name":"Milic-Frayling, Natasa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OASIS: A Multilingual and Multimodal Dataset for Culturally Grounded Spoken Visual QA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.2.2; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:37:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:04:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-09T06:32:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T00:32:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T05:22:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-13T00:35:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale multimodal models achieve strong results on tasks like Visual Question Answering (VQA), but they are often limited when queries require cultural and visual information, everyday knowledge, particularly in low-resource and underrepresented languages. We introduce OASIS, a large-scale culturally grounded multimodal QA dataset covering images, text, and speech. OASIS is built with EverydayMMQA, a scalable semi-automatic framework for creating localized spoken and visual QA resources, supported by multi-stage human-in-the-loop validation. OASIS contains approximately 0.92M real images and 14.8M QA pairs, including 3.7M spoken questions, with 383 hours of human-recorded speech, and 20K hours of voice-cloned speech, from 42 speakers. It supports four input settings: text-only, speech-only, text+image, and speech+image. The dataset focuses on English and Arabic varieties across 18 countries, covering Modern Standard Arabic (MSA) as well as dialectal Arabic. It is designed to evaluate models beyond object recognition, targeting pragmatic, commonsense, and culturally grounded reasoning in real-world scenarios. We benchmark four closed-source models, three open-source models, and one fine-tuned model on OASIS. The framework and dataset will be made publicly available to the community. https://huggingface.co/datasets/QCRI/OASIS"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Multimodal Foundation Models, Large Language Models, Native, Multilingual, Language Diversity, Contextual Understanding, Culturally Informed"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:44Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06637","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06637","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06637","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prakhar","familyName":"Srivastava","name":"Srivastava, Prakhar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farrin Marouf","familyName":"Sofian","name":"Sofian, Farrin Marouf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Immorlano","name":"Immorlano, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kushagra","familyName":"Pandey","name":"Pandey, Kushagra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephan","familyName":"Mandt","name":"Mandt, Stephan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Control-Augmented Autoregressive Diffusion for Data Assimilation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T04:37:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:23:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-17T12:05:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-18T01:39:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-10T00:27:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:01:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite advances in test-time scaling and diffusion finetuning, guidance for Auto-Regressive Diffusion Models (ARDMs) remains underexplored. We introduce an amortized framework that augments a pretrained ARDM with an offline-trained controller. By previewing future rollouts, the controller learns stepwise corrections that anticipate observations under a terminal-cost objective, yielding a reusable policy for guided generation. Motivated by a stochastic optimal control view of ARDM trajectories, our method injects small controls within each denoising sub-step while staying close to the pretrained dynamics. We study this approach for dataassimilation (DA) in chaotic spatiotemporal partial differential equations (PDEs), where existing methods are often computationally expensive and susceptible to forecast drift under sparse observations. At inference, DA becomes a feed-forward rollout with on-the-fly corrections, achieving an order-of-magnitude speedup over strong diffusion-based baselines. Across two canonical PDEs and a compact ECMWF Reanalysis v5 (ERA5) pilot spanning six observation regimes, our method consistently improves stability and accuracy over state-of-the-art alternatives, with similar improvements observed in a larger-scale GenCast study."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06637","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:50:56Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:50:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:04:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07143","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07143","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07143","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenfei","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Chenfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wensong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wensong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichen","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haocong","familyName":"He","name":"He, Haocong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanhuiyi","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Yuanhuiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lutao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Lutao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqian","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yuqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuming","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are We Using the Right Benchmark: An Evaluation Framework for Visual Token Compression Methods"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:44:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:56:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-23T06:35:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:20:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T07:39:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:47:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent efforts to accelerate inference in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have largely focused on visual token compression. The effectiveness of these methods is commonly evaluated by measuring the accuracy drop on existing MLLM benchmarks before and after compression. However, these benchmarks are originally designed to assess general perception and reasoning abilities, rather than the specific challenges posed by visual token compression, leading to a fundamental task mismatch. In this work, we uncover a counterintuitive yet consistent phenomenon: simple image downsampling outperforms many advanced visual token compression methods across multiple widely used benchmarks. Through a comprehensive empirical study spanning eight popular benchmarks and multiple state-of-the-art compression techniques, we show that (i) current benchmarks contain substantial noise (task-irrelevant samples) for evaluating visual token compression, and (ii) downsampling can act as an effective data filter that distinguishes between simple and difficult samples with respect to compression sensitivity. Motivated by these findings, we propose VTC-Bench, an evaluation framework that explicitly leverages downsampling as a discriminator to denoise existing benchmarks, enabling a fairer and more meaningful additional assessment of visual token compression methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACL2026 Main. Code: https://github.com/Chenfei-Liao/VTC-Bench; Project Page: https://chenfei-liao.github.io/VTC-Bench-Page/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07143","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:02:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06687","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06687","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06687","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihui","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Yihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Geometry-Aware Cross Modal Alignment for Light Field-LiDAR Semantic Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T06:15:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T02:27:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-13T17:44:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T02:09:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T05:29:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-15T00:27:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-15T05:08:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-16T00:28:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Semantic segmentation serves as a cornerstone of scene understanding in autonomous driving but continues to face significant challenges under complex conditions such as occlusion. Light field and LiDAR modalities provide complementary visual and spatial cues that are beneficial for robust perception; however, their effective integration is hindered by limited viewpoint diversity and inherent modality discrepancies. To address these challenges, the first multimodal semantic segmentation dataset integrating light field data and point cloud data is proposed. Based on this dataset, we proposed a multi-modal light field point-cloud fusion segmentation network(Mlpfseg), incorporating feature completion and depth perception to segment both camera images and LiDAR point clouds simultaneously. The feature completion module addresses the density mismatch between point clouds and image pixels by performing differential reconstruction of point-cloud feature maps, enhancing the fusion of these modalities. The depth perception module improves the segmentation of occluded objects by reinforcing attention scores for better occlusion awareness. Our method outperforms image-only segmentation by 1.71 Mean Intersection over Union(mIoU) and point cloud-only segmentation by 2.38 mIoU, demonstrating its effectiveness."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06687","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:52:06Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:52:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-16T02:18:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06545","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06545","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06545","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacek","familyName":"Karwowski","name":"Karwowski, Jacek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raymond","familyName":"Douglas","name":"Douglas, Raymond","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Incoherence in Goal-Conditioned Autoregressive Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T00:52:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:15:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-01T11:58:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-02T00:47:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate mathematically the notion of incoherence: a structural issue with reinforcement learning policies derived by naive goal-conditioning of autoregressive models. We focus on the process of re-training models on their own actions, that is, fine-tuning offline-learned policies with online RL. We prove that it decreases incoherence and leads to an improvement in return, and we aim to characterize the resulting trajectory of policies. By re-framing standard notions of control-as-inference and soft Q learning, we establish a three-way correspondence with two other ways of understanding the iterative re-training process: as folding the posterior into the reward and, in the deterministic case, as decreasing the temperature parameter; the correspondence has computational content via the training-inference trade-off. Through soft-conditioning generative models, we discuss the link between incoherence and the effective horizon."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in the Proceedings of the 29th International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Statistics (AISTATS) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06545","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:48:46Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:48:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-02T02:28:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06790","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06790","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06790","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tavish","familyName":"McDonald","name":"McDonald, Tavish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stanislav","familyName":"Fort","name":"Fort, Stanislav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhavya","familyName":"Kailkhura","name":"Kailkhura, Bhavya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Bartoldson","name":"Bartoldson, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Get RICH or Die Scaling: Profitably Trading Inference Compute for Robustness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:18:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:35:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-01T18:15:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T02:48:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T04:07:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-27T00:26:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-time reasoning has raised benchmark performances and even shown promise in addressing the historically intractable problem of making models robust to adversarially out-of-distribution (OOD) data. Indeed, recent work used reasoning to aid satisfaction of model specifications designed to thwart attacks, finding a striking correlation between LLM reasoning effort and robustness to jailbreaks. However, this benefit fades when stronger (e.g. gradient-based or multimodal) attacks are used. This may be expected as models often can't follow instructions on the adversarially OOD data created by such attacks, and instruction following is needed to act in accordance with the attacker-thwarting spec. Thus, we hypothesize that the test-time robustness benefits of specs are unlocked by initial robustness sufficient to follow instructions on OOD data. Namely, we posit the Robustness from Inference Compute Hypothesis (RICH): inference-compute defenses profit as the model's training data better reflects the components of attacked data. Guided by the RICH, we test models of varying initial-robustness levels, finding inference-compute adds robustness even to white-box multimodal attacks, provided the model has sufficient initial robustness. Further evidencing a rich-get-richer dynamic, InternVL 3.5 gpt-oss 20B gains little robustness when its test compute is scaled, but such scaling adds significant robustness if we first robustify its vision encoder (creating the first adversarially robust reasoning VLM in the process). Robustifying models makes attacked components of data more in-distribution (ID), and the RICH suggests this fuels compositional generalization -- understanding OOD data via its ID components -- to following spec instructions on adversarial data. Consistently, we find test-time defenses both build and depend on train-time data and defenses."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06790","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:30Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-27T02:22:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06638","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06638","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06638","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenkang","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Wenkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingtong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xingtong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weicheng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Weicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StaR-KVQA: Structured Reasoning Traces for Implicit-Knowledge Visual Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T04:37:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:23:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-15T09:26:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:29:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-22T14:23:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:09:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge-based Visual Question Answering (KVQA) requires models to ground entities in images and reason over factual knowledge. Recent work has introduced its implicit-knowledge variant, IK-KVQA, where a multimodal large language model (MLLM) is the sole knowledge source and answers are produced without external retrieval. Existing IK-KVQA approaches, however, are typically trained with answer-only supervision: reasoning remains implicit, justifications are often weak or inconsistent, and generalization after standard supervised fine-tuning (SFT) can be brittle. We propose StaR-KVQA, a framework that equips IK-KVQA with dual-path structured reasoning traces - symbolic relation paths over text and vision together with path-grounded natural-language explanations - to provide a stronger inductive bias than generic answer-only supervision. These traces act as modality-aware scaffolds that guide the model toward relevant entities and attributes, offering more structure than generic chain-of-thought supervision while not constraining reasoning to any single fixed path. With a single open-source MLLM, StaR-KVQA constructs and selects traces to build an offline trace-enriched dataset and then performs structure-aware self-distillation; no external retrievers, verifiers, or curated knowledge bases are used, and inference is a single autoregressive pass. Across benchmarks, StaR-KVQA consistently improves both answer accuracy and the transparency of intermediate reasoning, achieving up to +11.3% higher answer accuracy on OK-VQA over the strongest baseline."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8+3+3 pages, code: https://github.com/jianyingzhihe/StaR-KVQA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06638","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:50:57Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:50:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:17:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07181","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07181","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07181","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enshen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Enshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanyu","familyName":"Rong","name":"Rong, Shanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingkun","familyName":"An","name":"An, Jingkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanghang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shanghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TIGeR: Tool-Integrated Geometric Reasoning in Vision-Language Models for Robotics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:20:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:58:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T13:56:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:57:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T07:07:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-05T01:36:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have shown remarkable capabilities in spatial reasoning, yet they remain fundamentally limited to qualitative precision and lack the computational precision required for real-world robotics. Current approaches fail to leverage metric cues from depth sensors and camera calibration, instead reducing geometric problems to pattern recognition tasks that cannot deliver the centimeter-level accuracy essential for robotic manipulation. We present TIGeR (Tool-Integrated Geometric Reasoning), a novel framework that transforms VLMs from perceptual estimators to geometric computers by enabling them to generate and execute precise geometric computations through external tools. Rather than attempting to internalize complex geometric operations within neural networks, TIGeR empowers models to recognize geometric reasoning requirements, synthesize appropriate computational code, and invoke specialized libraries for exact calculations. To support this paradigm, we introduce TIGeR-300K, a comprehensive tool-invocation-oriented dataset covering point transformations, pose estimation, and spatial compatibility verification, complete with tool invocation sequences and intermediate computations. Through a two-stage training pipeline combining supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and reinforcement fine-tuning (RFT) with our proposed hierarchical reward design, TIGeR achieves SOTA performance on geometric reasoning benchmarks while demonstrating centimeter-level precision in real-world robotic manipulation tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07181","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:48Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-05T03:44:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06292","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06292","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06292","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yike","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ChainMPQ: Interleaved Text-Image Reasoning Chains for Mitigating Relation Hallucinations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:19:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-01T01:39:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:56:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) achieve strong performance in multimodal tasks, hallucinations continue to hinder their reliability. Among the three categories of hallucinations, which include object, attribute, and relation, relation hallucinations account for the largest proportion but have received the least attention. To address this issue, we propose ChainMPQ (Multi-Perspective Questions guided Interleaved Chain of Image and Text), a training-free method that improves relational inference in LVLMs by utilizing accumulated textual and visual memories. ChainMPQ first extracts subject and object keywords from the question to enhance the corresponding image regions. It then constructs multi-perspective questions that focus on the three core components of a relationship: the subject, the object, and the relation that links them. These questions are sequentially input to the model, with textual and visual memories from earlier steps providing supporting context for subsequent ones, thereby forming an interleaved chain of images and text that guides progressive relational reasoning. Experiments on multiple LVLMs and benchmarks show that ChainMPQ substantially reduces relation hallucinations, while ablation studies further validate the effectiveness of its three core modules."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06292","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06820","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06820","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06820","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mitchell Keren","familyName":"Taraday","name":"Taraday, Mitchell Keren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shahaf","familyName":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Shahaf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaim","familyName":"Baskin","name":"Baskin, Chaim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Discriminative Joint Encoders for Large Scale Vision-Language Reranking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:46:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:37:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-22T12:55:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal retrieval still leans on embedding-based models like CLIP for fast vector search over pre-computed image embeddings. Yet, unlike text retrieval, where joint-encoder rerankers are standard, comparable vision-language rerankers are largely absent. We find that seminal joint encoders such as BLIP are severely bottlenecked by an expensive visual feature-extraction stage, preventing practical deployment at scale. Motivated by this bottleneck, we introduce EDJE, an Efficient Discriminative Joint Encoder that precomputes vision tokens offline and compresses them via a lightweight attention-based adapter, so online inference runs only a compact joint encoder over a small set of visual tokens plus the text. EDJE preserves strong retrieval performance while drastically reducing storage and online compute, enabling high-throughput inference. Specifically, EDJE processes 50k image--text pairs/second while requiring 49kB of disk storage per image, matching prior art on Flickr (zero-shot) and COCO (fine-tuned) retrieval."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06820","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:12Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06751","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06751","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06751","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Junhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hesong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hesong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingluo","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Mingluo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zefang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zefang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OBS-Diff: Accurate Pruning For Diffusion Models in One-Shot"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T08:19:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:32:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-02T06:03:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-05T01:33:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-23T08:33:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T02:15:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale text-to-image diffusion models, while powerful, suffer from prohibitive computational cost. Existing one-shot network pruning methods can hardly be directly applied to them due to the iterative denoising nature of diffusion models. To bridge the gap, this paper presents OBS-Diff, a novel one-shot pruning framework that enables accurate and training-free compression of large-scale text-to-image diffusion models. Specifically, (i) OBS-Diff revitalizes the classic Optimal Brain Surgeon (OBS), adapting it to the complex architectures of modern diffusion models and supporting diverse pruning granularity, including unstructured, N:M semi-structured, and structured (MHA heads and FFN neurons) sparsity; (ii) To align the pruning criteria with the iterative dynamics of the diffusion process, by examining the problem from an error-accumulation perspective, we propose a novel timestep-aware Hessian construction that incorporates a logarithmic-decrease weighting scheme, assigning greater importance to earlier timesteps to mitigate potential error accumulation; (iii) Furthermore, a computationally efficient group-wise sequential pruning strategy is proposed to amortize the expensive calibration process. Extensive experiments show that OBS-Diff achieves state-of-the-art one-shot pruning for diffusion models, delivering inference acceleration with minimal degradation in visual quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06751","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:35Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06738","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06738","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06738","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyi","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Boyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinbing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhouhan","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhouhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AWM: Accurate Weight-Matrix Fingerprint for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:51:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:31:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-03T09:39:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-04T01:47:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-14T13:03:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-17T01:32:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Protecting the intellectual property of large language models (LLMs) is crucial, given the substantial resources required for their training. Consequently, there is an urgent need for both model owners and third parties to determine whether a suspect LLM is trained from scratch or derived from an existing base model. However, the intensive post-training processes that models typically undergo-such as supervised fine-tuning, extensive continued pretraining, reinforcement learning, multi-modal extension, pruning, and upcycling-pose significant challenges to reliable identification. In this work, we propose a training-free fingerprinting method based on weight matrices. We leverage the Linear Assignment Problem (LAP) and an unbiased Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) similarity to neutralize the effects of parameter manipulations, yielding a highly robust and high-fidelity similarity metric. On a comprehensive testbed of 60 positive and 90 negative model pairs, our method demonstrates exceptional robustness against all six aforementioned post-training categories while exhibiting a near-zero risk of false positives. By achieving perfect scores on all classification metrics, our approach establishes a strong basis for reliable model lineage verification. Moreover, the entire computation completes within 30s on an NVIDIA 3090 GPU. The code is available at https://github.com/LUMIA-Group/AWM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06738","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:18Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-17T04:46:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06980","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06980","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06980","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyi","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Xinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingxi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lizhen","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Lizhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongzhi","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Hongzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Relational Database Distillation: From Structured Tables to Condensed Graph Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:05:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:46:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-20T13:44:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T03:19:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Relational databases (RDBs) underpin the majority of global data management systems, where information is structured into multiple interdependent tables. To effectively use the knowledge within RDBs for predictive tasks, recent advances leverage graph representation learning to capture complex inter-table relations as multi-hop dependencies. Despite achieving state-of-the-art performance, these methods remain hindered by the prohibitive storage overhead and excessive training time, due to the massive scale of the database and the computational burden of intensive message passing across interconnected tables. To alleviate these concerns, we propose and study the problem of Relational Database Distillation (RDD). Specifically, we aim to distill large-scale RDBs into compact heterogeneous graphs while retaining the predictive power (i.e., utility) required for training graph-based models. Multi-modal column information is preserved through node features, and primary-foreign key relations are encoded via heterogeneous edges, thereby maintaining both data fidelity and relational structure. To ensure adaptability across diverse downstream tasks without engaging the traditional, inefficient bi-level distillation framework, we further design a kernel ridge regression-guided objective with pseudo-labels, which produces quality features for the distilled graph. Extensive experiments on multiple real-world RDBs demonstrate that our solution substantially reduces the data size while maintaining competitive performance on classification and regression tasks, creating an effective pathway for scalable learning with RDBs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06980","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:58:59Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:58:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06243","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06243","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06243","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qihua","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Qihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"Figueroa","name":"Figueroa, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Handong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Handong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kushal","familyName":"Kafle","name":"Kafle, Kushal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Kuen","name":"Kuen, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihong","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Zhihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Scott","familyName":"Cohen","name":"Cohen, Scott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CoT Referring: Improving Referring Expression Tasks with Grounded Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T08:50:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-17T09:14:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T01:23:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Referring Expression Comprehension and Segmentation are critical tasks for assessing the integration of language understanding and image comprehension, serving as benchmarks for Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) capabilities. To address these challenges, we propose a new strategy, CoT Referring, which enhances model reasoning across modalities through a structured, chain-of-thought training data structure. Our approach systematically parses textual structures to a sequential referring step, where in each step it identifies relationships and ensures consistent reference alignment, thereby improving accuracy in complex query scenarios. We restructure the training data to enforce a new output form, providing new annotations for existing datasets and compiling an evaluation benchmark from existing resources. This benchmark is designed explicitly for complex referring cases. We also integrate detection and segmentation capabilities into a unified MLLM framework, training it with a novel adaptive weighted loss to optimize performance. Experimental results on our curated benchmark and RefCOCO/+/g demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach, with a notable increase of 2.5%+ over baseline models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"MLLM, Referring Expression Segmentation"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06243","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:45Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07219","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07219","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07219","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhua","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yuhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengpei","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Chengpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jiaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingying","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jingying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Approximate Gaussian Mapping for Generative Image Steganography"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:53:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:59:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-13T13:44:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T01:33:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ordinary differential equation (ODE)-based diffusion models enable deterministic image synthesis, establishing a reversible mapping suitable for generative steganography. While prevailing methods strictly adhere to a standard normal prior, empirical evidence indicates that controlled deviations from this distribution reduce numerical inversion errors without compromising perceptual quality. Leveraging this observation, the Approximate Gaussian Mapping (AGM) is proposed as a linear transformation strategy that embeds secrets by modulating noise scale and variance. To balance retrieval numerical consistence and security, a two-stage decoupled optimization strategy is introduced to minimize the Kullback-Leibler divergence subject to target bit accuracy constraints. Beyond the proposed method, we conduct a mechanistic analysis of the divergent behaviors between pixel-space and latent-space architectures. The experimental results reveal that the VAE encoder enhances robustness by filtering external perturbations, whereas the structural regularization of the VAE decoder and the semantic variance introduced by text prompts jointly mask embedding artifacts to improve security. Experimental results confirm that pixel-space mplementations maximize embedding capacity for lossless channels, while latent-space approaches offer superior robustness and security suitable for adversarial environments"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07219","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:41Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:50:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06249","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06249","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06249","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toshiki","familyName":"Nakai","name":"Nakai, Toshiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ravi Kiran","familyName":"Chikkala","name":"Chikkala, Ravi Kiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lena Sophie","familyName":"Oberkircher","name":"Oberkircher, Lena Sophie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Jennings","name":"Jennings, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natalia","familyName":"Skachkova","name":"Skachkova, Natalia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatiana","familyName":"Anikina","name":"Anikina, Tatiana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesujoba Oluwadara","familyName":"Alabi","name":"Alabi, Jesujoba Oluwadara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TRepLiNa: Layer-wise CKA+REPINA Alignment Improves Low-Resource Machine Translation in Aya-23 8B"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T17:36:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T15:03:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:39:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-28T19:06:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:04:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-08T16:04:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-09T02:40:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-10T18:15:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-12-11T01:54:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The 2025 Multimodal Models for Low-Resource Contexts and Social Impact (MMLoSo) Language Challenge addresses one of India's most pressing linguistic gaps: the lack of resources for its diverse low-resource languages (LRLs). In this study, we investigate whether enforcing cross-lingual similarity in specific internal layers of a decoder-only multilingual large language model (LLM) can improve translation quality from LRL to high-resource language (HRL). Specifically, we combine Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA), a similarity metric that encourages representations of different languages to align, with REPINA, a regularization method that constrains parameter updates to remain close to the pretrained model, into a joint method we call TRepLiNa. In this research project, we experiment with zero-shot, few-shot, and fine-tuning settings using Aya-23 8B with QLoRA across MMLoSo shared task language pairs (Mundari, Santali, Bhili) with Hindi/English pivots. Our results show that aligning mid-level layers using TRepLiNa (CKA+REPINA) is a low-cost, practical approach to improving LRL translation, especially in data-scarce settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"It is work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06249","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-11T03:05:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06791","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06791","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06791","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changlin","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Changlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunzhong","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Yunzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael Randall","familyName":"Barnes","name":"Barnes, Michael Randall","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Shome","name":"Shome, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dylan","familyName":"Campbell","name":"Campbell, Dylan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Extreme Amodal Face Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:22:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:35:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-06T09:30:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:24:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Extreme amodal detection is the task of inferring the 2D location of objects that are not fully visible in the input image but are visible within an expanded field-of-view. This differs from amodal detection, where the object is partially visible within the input image, but is occluded. In this paper, we consider the sub-problem of face detection, since this class provides motivating applications involving safety and privacy, but do not tailor our method specifically to this class. Existing approaches rely on image sequences so that missing detections may be interpolated from surrounding frames or make use of generative models to sample possible completions. In contrast, we consider the single-image task and propose a more efficient, sample-free approach that makes use of the contextual cues from the image to infer the presence of unseen faces. We design a heatmap-based extreme amodal object detector that addresses the problem of efficiently predicting a lot (the out-of-frame region) from a little (the image) with a selective coarse-to-fine decoder. Our method establishes strong results for this new task, even outperforming less efficient generative approaches. Code, data, and models are available at https://charliesong1999.github.io/exaft_web/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by WACV 2026, project page: https://charliesong1999.github.io/exaft_web/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06791","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:32Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-09T04:37:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06783","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06783","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06783","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshit","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Akshit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shyam","familyName":"Marjit","name":"Marjit, Shyam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Gavrikov","name":"Gavrikov, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Serena","familyName":"Yeung-Levy","name":"Yeung-Levy, Serena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hilde","familyName":"Kuehne","name":"Kuehne, Hilde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rogerio","familyName":"Feris","name":"Feris, Rogerio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sivan","familyName":"Doveh","name":"Doveh, Sivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Glass","name":"Glass, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. Jehanzeb","familyName":"Mirza","name":"Mirza, M. Jehanzeb","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TTRV: Test-Time Reinforcement Learning for Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:10:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:35:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-04T13:17:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-05T01:41:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing methods for extracting reward signals in Reinforcement Learning typically rely on labeled data and dedicated training splits, a setup that contrasts with how humans learn directly from their environment. In this work, we propose TTRV to enhance vision language understanding by adapting the model on the fly at inference time, without the need for any labeled data. Concretely, we enhance the Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) framework by designing rewards based on the frequency of the base model's output, while inferring on each test sample multiple times. Further, we also propose to control the diversity of the model's output by simultaneously rewarding the model for obtaining low entropy of the output empirical distribution. Our approach delivers consistent gains across both object recognition and visual question answering (VQA), with improvements of up to 52.4% and 29.8%, respectively, and average boosts of 24.6% and 10.0% across 16 datasets. Remarkably, on image recognition, TTRV applied to InternVL 8B surpasses GPT-4o by an average of 2.3% over 8 benchmarks, while remaining highly competitive on VQA, demonstrating that test-time reinforcement learning can match or exceed the strongest proprietary models. Finally, we find many interesting properties of test-time RL for VLMs: for example, even in extremely data-constrained scenarios, where adaptation is performed on a single randomly chosen unlabeled test example, TTRV still yields non-trivial improvements of up to 5.5% in recognition tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06783","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:20Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-05T03:07:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06251","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06251","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06251","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ieva","familyName":"Bagdonaviciute","name":"Bagdonaviciute, Ieva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vibhav","familyName":"Vineet","name":"Vineet, Vibhav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physics Knowledge in Frontier Models: A Diagnostic Study of Failure Modes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T22:30:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-14T21:33:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:11:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While recent Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have achieved impressive progress, it remains difficult to determine why they succeed or fail on complex reasoning tasks. Traditional benchmarks evaluate what models can answer correctly, not why they succeed or fail. In this work, we perform a failure-mode analysis of six frontier VLMs on three physics-based benchmarks - Physion, Physion++, and CLEVRER - by introducing custom subtests (for Physion and Physion++) and an integration of existing benchmark categories (for CLEVRER) to factor benchmark performance into distinct, testable capabilities. These subtests isolate perception (object, color, and occlusion recognition) and physics understanding (motion prediction and spatial reasoning), enabling us to test whether models attend to the correct entities and dynamics underlying their answers. Counterintuitively, subtest mastery correlates only weakly with benchmark accuracy: models often answer correctly without grounding in perception or physics. This suggests that current VLMs sometimes achieve benchmark scores for the wrong reasons, underscoring the need for diagnostics that expose hidden failure modes beyond aggregate metrics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 7 figures. Preprint. v2: Updated experiments and diagnostics; formatting fixes"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06251","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:56Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:16:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07316","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07316","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07316","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gangwei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Gangwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotong","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Haotong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongcheng","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Hongcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xianqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingfeng","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Jingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianghui","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Lianghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuechuan","familyName":"Pu","name":"Pu, Yuechuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiyang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Haiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hangjun","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Hangjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sida","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Sida","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pixel-Perfect Depth with Semantics-Prompted Diffusion Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:59:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:04:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T02:15:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:21:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents Pixel-Perfect Depth, a monocular depth estimation model based on pixel-space diffusion generation that produces high-quality, flying-pixel-free point clouds from estimated depth maps. Current generative depth estimation models fine-tune Stable Diffusion and achieve impressive performance. However, they require a VAE to compress depth maps into latent space, which inevitably introduces \\textit{flying pixels} at edges and details. Our model addresses this challenge by directly performing diffusion generation in the pixel space, avoiding VAE-induced artifacts. To overcome the high complexity associated with pixel-space generation, we introduce two novel designs: 1) Semantics-Prompted Diffusion Transformers (SP-DiT), which incorporate semantic representations from vision foundation models into DiT to prompt the diffusion process, thereby preserving global semantic consistency while enhancing fine-grained visual details; and 2) Cascade DiT Design that progressively increases the number of tokens to further enhance efficiency and accuracy. Our model achieves the best performance among all published generative models across five benchmarks, and significantly outperforms all other models in edge-aware point cloud evaluation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025. Project page: https://pixel-perfect-depth.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07316","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:07:01Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:07:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-30T02:18:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06259","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06259","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06259","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jahidul","familyName":"Arafat","name":"Arafat, Jahidul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fariha","familyName":"Tasmin","name":"Tasmin, Fariha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjaya","familyName":"Poudel","name":"Poudel, Sanjaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iftekhar","familyName":"Haider","name":"Haider, Iftekhar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Static Knowledge Messengers: Towards Adaptive, Fair, and Scalable Federated Learning for Medical AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.11; K.4.1; J.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T05, 62P10, 68T07","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T03:07:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-23T08:34:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:35:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical AI faces challenges in privacy-preserving collaborative learning while ensuring fairness across heterogeneous healthcare institutions. Current federated learning approaches suffer from static architectures, slow convergence (45-73 rounds), fairness gaps marginalizing smaller institutions, and scalability constraints (15-client limit). We propose Adaptive Fair Federated Learning (AFFL) through three innovations: (1) Adaptive Knowledge Messengers dynamically scaling capacity based on heterogeneity and task complexity, (2) Fairness-Aware Distillation using influence-weighted aggregation, and (3) Curriculum-Guided Acceleration reducing rounds by 60-70%. Our theoretical analysis provides convergence guarantees with epsilon-fairness bounds, achieving O(T^{-1/2}) + O(H_max/T^{3/4}) rates. Projected results show 55-75% communication reduction, 56-68% fairness improvement, 34-46% energy savings, and 100+ institution support. The framework enables multi-modal integration across imaging, genomics, EHR, and sensor data while maintaining HIPAA/GDPR compliance. We propose MedFedBench benchmark suite for standardized evaluation across six healthcare dimensions: convergence efficiency, institutional fairness, privacy preservation, multi-modal integration, scalability, and clinical deployment readiness. Economic projections indicate 400-800% ROI for rural hospitals and 15-25% performance gains for academic centers. This work presents a seven-question research agenda, 24-month implementation roadmap, and pathways toward democratizing healthcare AI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 4 figures, 14 tables. Proposes Adaptive Fair Federated Learning (AFFL) algorithm and MedFedBench benchmark suite for healthcare federated learning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06259","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:07Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-24T02:15:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06293","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06293","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06293","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cristian","familyName":"Meo","name":"Meo, Cristian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varun","familyName":"Sarathchandran","name":"Sarathchandran, Varun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avijit","familyName":"Majhi","name":"Majhi, Avijit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shao","familyName":"Hung","name":"Hung, Shao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlo","familyName":"Saccardi","name":"Saccardi, Carlo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruben","familyName":"Imhoff","name":"Imhoff, Ruben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberto","familyName":"Deidda","name":"Deidda, Roberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Remko","familyName":"Uijlenhoet","name":"Uijlenhoet, Remko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Justin","familyName":"Dauwels","name":"Dauwels, Justin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BlockGPT: Spatio-Temporal Modelling of Rainfall via Frame-Level Autoregression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T11:52:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-22T15:14:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-23T00:55:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predicting precipitation maps is a highly complex spatiotemporal modeling task, critical for mitigating the impacts of extreme weather events. Short-term precipitation forecasting, or nowcasting, requires models that are not only accurate but also computationally efficient for real-time applications. Current methods, such as token-based autoregressive models, often suffer from flawed inductive biases and slow inference, while diffusion models can be computationally intensive. To address these limitations, we introduce BlockGPT, a generative autoregressive transformer using batched tokenization (Block) method that predicts full two-dimensional fields (frames) at each time step. Conceived as a model-agnostic paradigm for video prediction, BlockGPT factorizes space-time by using self-attention within each frame and causal attention across frames; in this work, we instantiate it for precipitation nowcasting. We evaluate BlockGPT on two precipitation datasets, viz. KNMI (Netherlands) and SEVIR (U.S.), comparing it to state-of-the-art baselines including token-based (NowcastingGPT) and diffusion-based (DiffCast+Phydnet) models. The results show that BlockGPT achieves superior accuracy, event localization as measured by categorical metrics, and inference speeds up to 31x faster than comparable baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06293","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:55Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-23T02:17:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06303","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06303","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06303","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongbo","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Zhongbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qipeng","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biqing","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Biqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SDAR: A Synergistic Diffusion-AutoRegression Paradigm for Scalable Sequence Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:29:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:02:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T02:55:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:23:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-18T20:08:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:38:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose SDAR, a Synergistic Diffusion-Autoregression paradigm that unifies the training efficiency of autoregressive models with the parallel inference capability of diffusion. Instead of costly end-to-end diffusion training, SDAR performs a lightweight paradigm conversion that transforms a well-trained autoregressive (AR) model into a blockwise diffusion model through brief, data-efficient adaptation. During inference, SDAR generates sequences autoregressively across blocks for global coherence while decoding all tokens within each block in parallel via a discrete diffusion process. Extensive experiments show that AR models remain substantially more compute-efficient than masked diffusion models, providing a strong foundation for adaptation. Building on this insight, SDAR achieves efficient AR-to-diffusion conversion with minimal cost, preserving AR-level performance while enabling parallel generation. Scaling studies across dense and Mixture-of-Experts architectures confirm that SDAR scales without compromise: larger models exhibit stronger robustness to block size and decoding thresholds, yielding greater speedups without accuracy loss. Beyond efficiency, SDAR demonstrates enhanced reasoning and domain adaptability. Our 30B MoE model surpasses its AR counterpart on challenging scientific reasoning benchmarks such as GPQA and ChemBench, and gains further improvements under test-time scaling methods like majority voting and pass@k. Together, these results establish SDAR as a practical paradigm that combines the strengths of autoregression and diffusion for scalable, high-throughput reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Technical report. 40 pages, Inference speedup analysis added"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06303","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:43:09Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:43:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:42:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07249","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07249","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07249","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaben","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiaben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ailing","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Ailing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueyang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xueyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Cen","name":"Cen, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"Tanke","name":"Tanke, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koichi","familyName":"Saito","name":"Saito, Koichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Mitsufuji","name":"Mitsufuji, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuang","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Chuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TalkCuts: A Large-Scale Dataset for Multi-Shot Human Speech Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:16:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:01:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T02:46:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:25:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we present TalkCuts, a large-scale dataset designed to facilitate the study of multi-shot human speech video generation. Unlike existing datasets that focus on single-shot, static viewpoints, TalkCuts offers 164k clips totaling over 500 hours of high-quality human speech videos with diverse camera shots, including close-up, half-body, and full-body views. The dataset includes detailed textual descriptions, 2D keypoints and 3D SMPL-X motion annotations, covering over 10k identities, enabling multimodal learning and evaluation. As a first attempt to showcase the value of the dataset, we present Orator, an LLM-guided multi-modal generation framework as a simple baseline, where the language model functions as a multi-faceted director, orchestrating detailed specifications for camera transitions, speaker gesticulations, and vocal modulation. This architecture enables the synthesis of coherent long-form videos through our integrated multi-modal video generation module. Extensive experiments in both pose-guided and audio-driven settings show that training on TalkCuts significantly enhances the cinematographic coherence and visual appeal of generated multi-shot speech videos. We believe TalkCuts provides a strong foundation for future work in controllable, multi-shot speech video generation and broader multimodal learning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://talkcuts.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07249","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:24Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:24:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06509","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06509","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06509","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shih-Yao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Shih-Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sibendu","familyName":"Paul","name":"Paul, Sibendu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caren","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Caren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Captions to Keyframes: KeyScore for Multimodal Frame Scoring and Video-Language Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T23:02:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:12:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T07:42:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:30:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Selecting informative keyframes is critical for efficient video understanding, yet existing approaches often rely on heuristics, ignore semantics, or produce redundant frames. We propose KeyScore, a caption-aware frame scoring method that combines three complementary signals: semantic similarity to captions, temporal representativeness, and contextual drop impact. Applied to large-scale video-caption datasets, KeyScore generates frame-level importance scores that enable training keyframe extractors or guiding video-language models. To support this, we also propose STACFP, a Spatio-Temporal Adaptive Clustering method that generates diverse and compact frame proposals across long videos. Together, KeyScore and STACFP reduce uninformative frames while preserving critical content, resulting in faster and more accurate inference. Our experiments on three standard video-language benchmarks (MSRVTT, MSVD, DiDeMo) show that combining STACFP and KeyScore enables up to 99% frame reduction compared to full-frame processing, while outperforming uniform 8-frame encoders in video-text retrieval, keyframe extraction, and action recognition tasks. By focusing on semantically relevant frames, our method enhances both efficiency and performance, enabling scalable and caption-grounded video understanding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06509","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:47:56Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:47:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:18:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06871","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06871","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06871","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huahui","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Huahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiankun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qiankun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Miao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gongli","familyName":"Xi","name":"Xi, Gongli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuming","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SaFeR-VLM: Toward Safety-aware Fine-grained Reasoning in Multimodal Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:39:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:40:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T13:46:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:57:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Reasoning Models (MLRMs) demonstrate impressive cross-modal reasoning but often amplify safety risks under adversarial or unsafe prompts, a phenomenon we call the \\textit{Reasoning Tax}. Existing defenses mainly act at the output level and do not constrain the reasoning process, leaving models exposed to implicit risks. In this paper, we propose SaFeR-VLM, a safety-aligned reinforcement learning framework that embeds safety directly into multimodal reasoning. The framework integrates four components: (I) QI-Safe-10K, a curated dataset emphasizing safety-critical and reasoning-sensitive cases; (II) safety-aware rollout, where unsafe generations undergo reflection and correction instead of being discarded; (III) structured reward modeling with multi-dimensional weighted criteria and explicit penalties for hallucinations and contradictions; and (IV) GRPO optimization, which reinforces both safe and corrected trajectories. This unified design shifts safety from a passive safeguard to an active driver of reasoning, enabling scalable and generalizable safety-aware reasoning. SaFeR-VLM further demonstrates robustness against both explicit and implicit risks, supporting dynamic and interpretable safety decisions beyond surface-level filtering. SaFeR-VLM-3B achieves average performance $70.13$ and $78.97$ on safety and helpfulness across six benchmarks, surpassing both same-scale and $\u0026gt;10\\times$ larger models such as Skywork-R1V3-38B, Qwen2.5VL-72B, and GLM4.5V-106B. Remarkably, SaFeR-VLM-7B benefits from its increased scale to surpass GPT-5-mini and Gemini-2.5-Flash by \\num{6.47} and \\num{16.76} points respectively on safety metrics, achieving this improvement without any degradation in helpfulness performance. Our codes are available at https://github.com/HarveyYi/SaFeR-VLM."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06871","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:56:25Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:56:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:40:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06223","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06223","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06223","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hans G. W.","familyName":"van Dam","name":"van Dam, Hans G. W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Multimodal GUI Architecture for Interfacing with LLM-Based Conversational Assistants"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; D.2.11","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-31T14:40:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T12:55:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:53:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Advances in large language models (LLMs) and real-time speech recognition now make it possible to issue any graphical user interface (GUI) action through natural language and receive the corresponding system response directly through the GUI. Most production applications were never designed with speech in mind. This article provides a concrete architecture that enables GUIs to interface with LLM-based speech-enabled assistants.\n The architecture makes an application's navigation graph and semantics available through the Model Context Protocol (MCP). The ViewModel, part of the MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel) pattern, exposes the application's capabilities to the assistant by supplying both tools applicable to a currently visible view and application-global tools extracted from the GUI tree router. This architecture facilitates full voice accessibility while ensuring reliable alignment between spoken input and the visual interface, accompanied by consistent feedback across modalities. It future-proofs apps for upcoming OS super assistants that employ computer use agents (CUAs) and natively consume MCP if an application provides it.\n To address concerns about privacy and data security, the practical effectiveness of locally deployable, open-weight LLMs for speech-enabled multimodal UIs is evaluated. Findings suggest that recent smaller open-weight models approach the performance of leading proprietary models in overall accuracy and require enterprise-grade hardware for fast responsiveness.\n A demo implementation of the proposed architecture can be found at https://github.com/hansvdam/langbar"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 19 figures, code available at https://github.com/hansvdam/langbar"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06223","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:17Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07313","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07313","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07313","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zezhong","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Zezhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowei","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Xiaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shizun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shizun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaozhu","familyName":"Ju","name":"Ju, Xiaozhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sirui","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Sirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanghang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shanghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WristWorld: Generating Wrist-Views via 4D World Models for Robotic Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:59:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:04:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Wrist-view observations are crucial for VLA models as they capture fine-grained hand-object interactions that directly enhance manipulation performance. Yet large-scale datasets rarely include such recordings, resulting in a substantial gap between abundant anchor views and scarce wrist views. Existing world models cannot bridge this gap, as they require a wrist-view first frame and thus fail to generate wrist-view videos from anchor views alone. Amid this gap, recent visual geometry models such as VGGT emerge with geometric and cross-view priors that make it possible to address extreme viewpoint shifts. Inspired by these insights, we propose WristWorld, the first 4D world model that generates wrist-view videos solely from anchor views. WristWorld operates in two stages: (i) Reconstruction, which extends VGGT and incorporates our Spatial Projection Consistency (SPC) Loss to estimate geometrically consistent wrist-view poses and 4D point clouds; (ii) Generation, which employs our video generation model to synthesize temporally coherent wrist-view videos from the reconstructed perspective. Experiments on Droid, Calvin, and Franka Panda demonstrate state-of-the-art video generation with superior spatial consistency, while also improving VLA performance, raising the average task completion length on Calvin by 3.81% and closing 42.4% of the anchor-wrist view gap."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07313","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:56Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:06:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07299","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07299","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07299","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Plantinga","name":"Plantinga, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roozbeh","familyName":"Sattari","name":"Sattari, Roozbeh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karine","familyName":"Marcotte","name":"Marcotte, Karine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carla","familyName":"Di Gironimo","name":"Di Gironimo, Carla","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Madeleine","familyName":"Sharp","name":"Sharp, Madeleine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liziane","familyName":"Bouvier","name":"Bouvier, Liziane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maiya","familyName":"Geddes","name":"Geddes, Maiya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ingrid","familyName":"Verduyckt","name":"Verduyckt, Ingrid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Étienne","familyName":"de Villers-Sidani","name":"de Villers-Sidani, Étienne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mirco","familyName":"Ravanelli","name":"Ravanelli, Mirco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Denise","familyName":"Klein","name":"Klein, Denise","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Comparison of Speech Tasks in Human Expert and Machine Detection of Parkinson's Disease"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:54:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:03:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The speech of people with Parkinson's Disease (PD) has been shown to hold important clues about the presence and progression of the disease. We investigate the factors based on which humans experts make judgments of the presence of disease in speech samples over five different speech tasks: phonations, sentence repetition, reading, recall, and picture description. We make comparisons by conducting listening tests to determine clinicians accuracy at recognizing signs of PD from audio alone, and we conduct experiments with a machine learning system for detection based on Whisper. Across tasks, Whisper performs on par or better than human experts when only audio is available, especially on challenging but important subgroups of the data: younger patients, mild cases, and female patients. Whisper's ability to recognize acoustic cues in difficult cases complements the multimodal and contextual strengths of human experts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to SMASH 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07299","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:37Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:06:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07293","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07293","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07293","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peize","familyName":"He","name":"He, Peize","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichen","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yubo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaoqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jiajie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehui","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Zehui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuangcheng","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Zhuangcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangqi","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Xiangqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiabing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiabing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cunxiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Cunxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Conghui","familyName":"He","name":"He, Conghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AudioMarathon: A Comprehensive Benchmark for Long-Context Audio Understanding and Efficiency in Audio LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:50:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:03:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Processing long-form audio is a major challenge for Large Audio Language models (LALMs). These models struggle with the quadratic cost of attention ($O(N^2)$) and with modeling long-range temporal dependencies. Existing audio benchmarks are built mostly from short clips and do not evaluate models in realistic long context settings. To address this gap, we introduce AudioMarathon, a benchmark designed to evaluate both understanding and inference efficiency on long-form audio. AudioMarathon provides a diverse set of tasks built upon three pillars: long-context audio inputs with durations ranging from 90.0 to 300.0 seconds, which correspond to encoded sequences of 2,250 to 7,500 audio tokens, respectively, full domain coverage across speech, sound, and music, and complex reasoning that requires multi-hop inference. We evaluate state-of-the-art LALMs and observe clear performance drops as audio length grows. We also study acceleration techniques and analyze the trade-offs of token pruning and KV cache eviction. The results show large gaps across current LALMs and highlight the need for better temporal reasoning and memory-efficient architectures. We believe AudioMarathon will drive the audio and multimodal research community to develop more advanced audio understanding models capable of solving complex audio tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages, 23 figures, the code is available at \\url{https://github.com/DabDans/AudioMarathon}"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07293","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:28Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:06:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07206","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07206","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07206","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shirin","familyName":"Shoushtari","name":"Shoushtari, Shirin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. Salman","familyName":"Asif","name":"Asif, M. Salman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ulugbek S.","familyName":"Kamilov","name":"Kamilov, Ulugbek S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EigenScore: OOD Detection using Covariance in Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:42:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:59:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Out-of-distribution (OOD) detection is critical for the safe deployment of machine learning systems in safety-sensitive domains. Diffusion models have recently emerged as powerful generative models, capable of capturing complex data distributions through iterative denoising. Building on this progress, recent work has explored their potential for OOD detection. We propose EigenScore, a new OOD detection method that leverages the eigenvalue spectrum of the posterior covariance induced by a diffusion model. We argue that posterior covariance provides a consistent signal of distribution shift, leading to larger trace and leading eigenvalues on OOD inputs, yielding a clear spectral signature. We further provide analysis explicitly linking posterior covariance to distribution mismatch, establishing it as a reliable signal for OOD detection. To ensure tractability, we adopt a Jacobian-free subspace iteration method to estimate the leading eigenvalues using only forward evaluations of the denoiser. Empirically, EigenScore achieves SOTA performance, with up to 5% AUROC improvement over the best baseline. Notably, it remains robust in near-OOD settings such as CIFAR-10 vs CIFAR-100, where existing diffusion-based methods often fail."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07206","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:23Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:04:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07190","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07190","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07190","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Zhi","name":"Zhi, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenghong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chenghong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongjie","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Hongjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xihe","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xihe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengwentai","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Zhengwentai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Cun","name":"Cun, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wensen","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Wensen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoguang","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Xiaoguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MV-Performer: Taming Video Diffusion Model for Faithful and Synchronized Multi-view Performer Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:24:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:58:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent breakthroughs in video generation, powered by large-scale datasets and diffusion techniques, have shown that video diffusion models can function as implicit 4D novel view synthesizers. Nevertheless, current methods primarily concentrate on redirecting camera trajectory within the front view while struggling to generate 360-degree viewpoint changes. In this paper, we focus on human-centric subdomain and present MV-Performer, an innovative framework for creating synchronized novel view videos from monocular full-body captures. To achieve a 360-degree synthesis, we extensively leverage the MVHumanNet dataset and incorporate an informative condition signal. Specifically, we use the camera-dependent normal maps rendered from oriented partial point clouds, which effectively alleviate the ambiguity between seen and unseen observations. To maintain synchronization in the generated videos, we propose a multi-view human-centric video diffusion model that fuses information from the reference video, partial rendering, and different viewpoints. Additionally, we provide a robust inference procedure for in-the-wild video cases, which greatly mitigates the artifacts induced by imperfect monocular depth estimation. Extensive experiments on three datasets demonstrate our MV-Performer's state-of-the-art effectiveness and robustness, setting a strong model for human-centric 4D novel view synthesis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by SIGGRAPH Asia 2025 conference track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07190","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:00Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:04:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07138","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07138","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07138","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["CMAP, MERGE"],"givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Bansaye","name":"Bansaye, Vincent","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["LJLL, CMAP"],"givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Bertolino","name":"Bertolino, Alexandre","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["LJLL"],"givenName":"Ayman","familyName":"Moussa","name":"Moussa, Ayman","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stability of non-conservative cross diffusion model and approximation by stochastic particle systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:35:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:55:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the stability of non-conservative deterministic cross diffusion models and prove that they are approximated by stochastic population models when the populations become locally large. In this model, the individuals of two species move, reproduce and die with rates sensitive to the local densities of the two species. Quantitative estimates are given and convergence is obtained soon as the population per site and the number of sites go to infinity. The proofs rely on the extension of stability estimates via duality approach under a smallness condition and the development of large deviation estimates for structured population models, which are of independent interest. The proofs also involve martingale estimates in H^{-1} and improve the approximation results in the conservative case as well."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07138","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:46Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:02:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:02:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07134","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07134","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07134","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiahang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunpeng","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yunpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiazhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Minghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shaoan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Kui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanjing","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Hanjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhibo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangwei","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Fangwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TrackVLA++: Unleashing Reasoning and Memory Capabilities in VLA Models for Embodied Visual Tracking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:29:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:55:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Embodied Visual Tracking (EVT) is a fundamental ability that underpins practical applications, such as companion robots, guidance robots and service assistants, where continuously following moving targets is essential. Recent advances have enabled language-guided tracking in complex and unstructured scenes. However, existing approaches lack explicit spatial reasoning and effective temporal memory, causing failures under severe occlusions or in the presence of similar-looking distractors. To address these challenges, we present TrackVLA++, a novel Vision-Language-Action (VLA) model that enhances embodied visual tracking with two key modules, a spatial reasoning mechanism and a Target Identification Memory (TIM). The reasoning module introduces a Chain-of-Thought paradigm, termed Polar-CoT, which infers the target's relative position and encodes it as a compact polar-coordinate token for action prediction. Guided by these spatial priors, the TIM employs a gated update strategy to preserve long-horizon target memory, ensuring spatiotemporal consistency and mitigating target loss during extended occlusions. Extensive experiments show that TrackVLA++ achieves state-of-the-art performance on public benchmarks across both egocentric and multi-camera settings. On the challenging EVT-Bench DT split, TrackVLA++ surpasses the previous leading approach by 5.1 and 12, respectively. Furthermore, TrackVLA++ exhibits strong zero-shot generalization, enabling robust real-world tracking in dynamic and occluded scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://pku-epic.github.io/TrackVLA-plus-plus-Web/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07134","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:40Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:02:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:02:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07129","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07129","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07129","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Cechnicka","name":"Cechnicka, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Baugh","name":"Baugh, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weitong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mischa","familyName":"Dombrowski","name":"Dombrowski, Mischa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johannes C.","familyName":"Paetzold","name":"Paetzold, Johannes C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Candice","familyName":"Roufosse","name":"Roufosse, Candice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernhard","familyName":"Kainz","name":"Kainz, Bernhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Graph Conditioned Diffusion for Controllable Histopathology Image Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:26:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:55:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DPMs) have set new standards in high-quality image synthesis. Yet, controlled generation remains challenging, particularly in sensitive areas such as medical imaging. Medical images feature inherent structure such as consistent spatial arrangement, shape or texture, all of which are critical for diagnosis. However, existing DPMs operate in noisy latent spaces that lack semantic structure and strong priors, making it difficult to ensure meaningful control over generated content. To address this, we propose graph-based object-level representations for Graph-Conditioned-Diffusion. Our approach generates graph nodes corresponding to each major structure in the image, encapsulating their individual features and relationships. These graph representations are processed by a transformer module and integrated into a diffusion model via the text-conditioning mechanism, enabling fine-grained control over generation. We evaluate this approach using a real-world histopathology use case, demonstrating that our generated data can reliably substitute for annotated patient data in downstream segmentation tasks. The code is available here."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07129","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:33Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:02:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:02:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07099","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07099","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07099","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Himanshu","familyName":"Choudhary","name":"Choudhary, Himanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arishi","familyName":"Orra","name":"Orra, Arishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manoj","familyName":"Thakur","name":"Thakur, Manoj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diffusion-Augmented Reinforcement Learning for Robust Portfolio Optimization under Stress Scenarios"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Finance (q-fin.CP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:56:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:52:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the ever-changing and intricate landscape of financial markets, portfolio optimisation remains a formidable challenge for investors and asset managers. Conventional methods often struggle to capture the complex dynamics of market behaviour and align with diverse investor preferences. To address this, we propose an innovative framework, termed Diffusion-Augmented Reinforcement Learning (DARL), which synergistically integrates Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DDPMs) with Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) for portfolio management. By leveraging DDPMs to generate synthetic market crash scenarios conditioned on varying stress intensities, our approach significantly enhances the robustness of training data. Empirical evaluations demonstrate that DARL outperforms traditional baselines, delivering superior risk-adjusted returns and resilience against unforeseen crises, such as the 2025 Tariff Crisis. This work offers a robust and practical methodology to bolster stress resilience in DRL-driven financial applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07099","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:01:51Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:01:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:01:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07098","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07098","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07098","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guo","familyName":"Yutong","name":"Yutong, Guo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanying","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wanying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichen","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Zichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TALENT: Table VQA via Augmented Language-Enhanced Natural-text Transcription"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:56:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:52:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Table Visual Question Answering (Table VQA) is typically addressed by large vision-language models (VLMs). While such models can answer directly from images, they often miss fine-grained details unless scaled to very large sizes, which are computationally prohibitive, especially for mobile deployment. A lighter alternative is to have a small VLM perform OCR and then use a large language model (LLM) to reason over structured outputs such as Markdown tables. However, these representations are not naturally optimized for LLMs and still introduce substantial errors. We propose TALENT (Table VQA via Augmented Language-Enhanced Natural-text Transcription), a lightweight framework that leverages dual representations of tables. TALENT prompts a small VLM to produce both OCR text and natural language narration, then combines them with the question for reasoning by an LLM. This reframes Table VQA as an LLM-centric multimodal reasoning task, where the VLM serves as a perception-narration module rather than a monolithic solver. Additionally, we construct ReTabVQA, a more challenging Table VQA dataset requiring multi-step quantitative reasoning over table images. Experiments show that TALENT enables a small VLM-LLM combination to match or surpass a single large VLM at significantly lower computational cost on both public datasets and ReTabVQA."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07098","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:01:49Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:01:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:01:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07092","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07092","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07092","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riccardo","familyName":"Mereu","name":"Mereu, Riccardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aidan","familyName":"Scannell","name":"Scannell, Aidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Jitta","name":"Jitta, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Dominguez","name":"Dominguez, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luigi","familyName":"Acerbi","name":"Acerbi, Luigi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amos","familyName":"Storkey","name":"Storkey, Amos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generative World Modelling for Humanoids: 1X World Model Challenge Technical Report"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:49:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:52:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"World models are a powerful paradigm in AI and robotics, enabling agents to reason about the future by predicting visual observations or compact latent states. The 1X World Model Challenge introduces an open-source benchmark of real-world humanoid interaction, with two complementary tracks: sampling, focused on forecasting future image frames, and compression, focused on predicting future discrete latent codes. For the sampling track, we adapt the video generation foundation model Wan-2.2 TI2V-5B to video-state-conditioned future frame prediction. We condition the video generation on robot states using AdaLN-Zero, and further post-train the model using LoRA. For the compression track, we train a Spatio-Temporal Transformer model from scratch. Our models achieve 23.0 dB PSNR in the sampling task and a Top-500 CE of 6.6386 in the compression task, securing 1st place in both challenges."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 3 figures, 1X world model challenge technical report"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07092","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:01:41Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:01:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:01:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07077","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07077","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07077","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kento","familyName":"Kawaharazuka","name":"Kawaharazuka, Kento","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihoon","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Jihoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Yamada","name":"Yamada, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ingmar","familyName":"Posner","name":"Posner, Ingmar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuke","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vision-Language-Action Models for Robotics: A Review Towards Real-World Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:38:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:51:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/access.2025.3609980","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Amid growing efforts to leverage advances in large language models (LLMs) and vision-language models (VLMs) for robotics, Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have recently gained significant attention. By unifying vision, language, and action data at scale, which have traditionally been studied separately, VLA models aim to learn policies that generalise across diverse tasks, objects, embodiments, and environments. This generalisation capability is expected to enable robots to solve novel downstream tasks with minimal or no additional task-specific data, facilitating more flexible and scalable real-world deployment. Unlike previous surveys that focus narrowly on action representations or high-level model architectures, this work offers a comprehensive, full-stack review, integrating both software and hardware components of VLA systems. In particular, this paper provides a systematic review of VLAs, covering their strategy and architectural transition, architectures and building blocks, modality-specific processing techniques, and learning paradigms. In addition, to support the deployment of VLAs in real-world robotic applications, we also review commonly used robot platforms, data collection strategies, publicly available datasets, data augmentation methods, and evaluation benchmarks. Throughout this comprehensive survey, this paper aims to offer practical guidance for the robotics community in applying VLAs to real-world robotic systems. All references categorized by training approach, evaluation method, modality, and dataset are available in the table on our project website: https://vla-survey.github.io ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to IEEE Access, website: https://vla-survey.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07077","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T02:01:19Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:01:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:01:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07067","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07067","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07067","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daria","familyName":"Pugacheva","name":"Pugacheva, Daria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrey","familyName":"Moskalenko","name":"Moskalenko, Andrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Denis","familyName":"Shepelev","name":"Shepelev, Denis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrey","familyName":"Kuznetsov","name":"Kuznetsov, Andrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vlad","familyName":"Shakhuro","name":"Shakhuro, Vlad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elena","familyName":"Tutubalina","name":"Tutubalina, Elena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bring the Apple, Not the Sofa: Impact of Irrelevant Context in Embodied AI Commands on VLA Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:31:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:51:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision Language Action (VLA) models are widely used in Embodied AI, enabling robots to interpret and execute language instructions. However, their robustness to natural language variability in real-world scenarios has not been thoroughly investigated. In this work, we present a novel systematic study of the robustness of state-of-the-art VLA models under linguistic perturbations. Specifically, we evaluate model performance under two types of instruction noise: (1) human-generated paraphrasing and (2) the addition of irrelevant context. We further categorize irrelevant contexts into two groups according to their length and their semantic and lexical proximity to robot commands. In this study, we observe consistent performance degradation as context size expands. We also demonstrate that the model can exhibit relative robustness to random context, with a performance drop within 10%, while semantically and lexically similar context of the same length can trigger a quality decline of around 50%. Human paraphrases of instructions lead to a drop of nearly 20%. To mitigate this, we propose an LLM-based filtering framework that extracts core commands from noisy inputs. Incorporating our filtering step allows models to recover up to 98.5% of their original performance under noisy conditions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07067","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:01:05Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:01:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:01:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07035","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07035","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07035","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tengwei","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Tengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unified Molecule Pre-training with Flexible 2D and 3D Modalities: Single and Paired Modality Integration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:02:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:49:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746252.3761084","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Molecular representation learning plays a crucial role in advancing applications such as drug discovery and material design. Existing work leverages 2D and 3D modalities of molecular information for pre-training, aiming to capture comprehensive structural and geometric insights. However, these methods require paired 2D and 3D molecular data to train the model effectively and prevent it from collapsing into a single modality, posing limitations in scenarios where a certain modality is unavailable or computationally expensive to generate. To overcome this limitation, we propose FlexMol, a flexible molecule pre-training framework that learns unified molecular representations while supporting single-modality input. Specifically, inspired by the unified structure in vision-language models, our approach employs separate models for 2D and 3D molecular data, leverages parameter sharing to improve computational efficiency, and utilizes a decoder to generate features for the missing modality. This enables a multistage continuous learning process where both modalities contribute collaboratively during training, while ensuring robustness when only one modality is available during inference. Extensive experiments demonstrate that FlexMol achieves superior performance across a wide range of molecular property prediction tasks, and we also empirically demonstrate its effectiveness with incomplete data. Our code and data are available at https://github.com/tewiSong/FlexMol."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CIKM 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07035","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:18Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:00:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07030","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07030","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07030","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhinav","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Abhinav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergio Aguilera","familyName":"Marinovic","name":"Marinovic, Sergio Aguilera","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soshi","familyName":"Iba","name":"Iba, Soshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rana Soltani","familyName":"Zarrin","name":"Zarrin, Rana Soltani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitry","familyName":"Berenson","name":"Berenson, Dmitry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diffusing Trajectory Optimization Problems for Recovery During Multi-Finger Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:58:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:49:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-fingered hands are emerging as powerful platforms for performing fine manipulation tasks, including tool use. However, environmental perturbations or execution errors can impede task performance, motivating the use of recovery behaviors that enable normal task execution to resume. In this work, we take advantage of recent advances in diffusion models to construct a framework that autonomously identifies when recovery is necessary and optimizes contact-rich trajectories to recover. We use a diffusion model trained on the task to estimate when states are not conducive to task execution, framed as an out-of-distribution detection problem. We then use diffusion sampling to project these states in-distribution and use trajectory optimization to plan contact-rich recovery trajectories. We also propose a novel diffusion-based approach that distills this process to efficiently diffuse the full parameterization, including constraints, goal state, and initialization, of the recovery trajectory optimization problem, saving time during online execution. We compare our method to a reinforcement learning baseline and other methods that do not explicitly plan contact interactions, including on a hardware screwdriver-turning task where we show that recovering using our method improves task performance by 96% and that ours is the only method evaluated that can attempt recovery without causing catastrophic task failure. Videos can be found at https://dtourrecovery.github.io/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07030","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:11Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:00:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06988","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06988","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06988","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Girolamo","familyName":"Macaluso","name":"Macaluso, Girolamo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Mandelli","name":"Mandelli, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mirko","familyName":"Bicchierai","name":"Bicchierai, Mirko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Berretti","name":"Berretti, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew D.","familyName":"Bagdanov","name":"Bagdanov, Andrew D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"No MoCap Needed: Post-Training Motion Diffusion Models with Reinforcement Learning using Only Textual Prompts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:12:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:46:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have recently advanced human motion generation, producing realistic and diverse animations from textual prompts. However, adapting these models to unseen actions or styles typically requires additional motion capture data and full retraining, which is costly and difficult to scale. We propose a post-training framework based on Reinforcement Learning that fine-tunes pretrained motion diffusion models using only textual prompts, without requiring any motion ground truth. Our approach employs a pretrained text-motion retrieval network as a reward signal and optimizes the diffusion policy with Denoising Diffusion Policy Optimization, effectively shifting the model's generative distribution toward the target domain without relying on paired motion data. We evaluate our method on cross-dataset adaptation and leave-one-out motion experiments using the HumanML3D and KIT-ML datasets across both latent- and joint-space diffusion architectures. Results from quantitative metrics and user studies show that our approach consistently improves the quality and diversity of generated motions, while preserving performance on the original distribution. Our approach is a flexible, data-efficient, and privacy-preserving solution for motion adaptation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06988","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:59:10Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:59:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:59:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06973","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06973","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06973","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhantao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huangji","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huangji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruili","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Ruili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuting","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yuting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangwen","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Shangwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Addressing the ID-Matching Challenge in Long Video Captioning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:59:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:46:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generating captions for long and complex videos is both critical and challenging, with significant implications for the growing fields of text-to-video generation and multi-modal understanding. One key challenge in long video captioning is accurately recognizing the same individuals who appear in different frames, which we refer to as the ID-Matching problem. Few prior works have focused on this important issue. Those that have, usually suffer from limited generalization and depend on point-wise matching, which limits their overall effectiveness. In this paper, unlike previous approaches, we build upon LVLMs to leverage their powerful priors. We aim to unlock the inherent ID-Matching capabilities within LVLMs themselves to enhance the ID-Matching performance of captions. Specifically, we first introduce a new benchmark for assessing the ID-Matching capabilities of video captions. Using this benchmark, we investigate LVLMs containing GPT-4o, revealing key insights that the performance of ID-Matching can be improved through two methods: 1) enhancing the usage of image information and 2) increasing the quantity of information of individual descriptions. Based on these insights, we propose a novel video captioning method called Recognizing Identities for Captioning Effectively (RICE). Extensive experiments including assessments of caption quality and ID-Matching performance, demonstrate the superiority of our approach. Notably, when implemented on GPT-4o, our RICE improves the precision of ID-Matching from 50% to 90% and improves the recall of ID-Matching from 15% to 80% compared to baseline. RICE makes it possible to continuously track different individuals in the captions of long videos."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06973","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:58:49Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:58:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:58:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06938","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06938","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06938","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuyen","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Tuyen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trong Nghia","familyName":"Hoang","name":"Hoang, Trong Nghia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Phi Le","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Phi Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hai L.","familyName":"Vu","name":"Vu, Hai L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Truong Cong","familyName":"Thang","name":"Thang, Truong Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Expressive and Scalable Quantum Fusion for Multimodal Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:19:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:44:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The aim of this paper is to introduce a quantum fusion mechanism for multimodal learning and to establish its theoretical and empirical potential. The proposed method, called the Quantum Fusion Layer (QFL), replaces classical fusion schemes with a hybrid quantum-classical procedure that uses parameterized quantum circuits to learn entangled feature interactions without requiring exponential parameter growth. Supported by quantum signal processing principles, the quantum component efficiently represents high-order polynomial interactions across modalities with linear parameter scaling, and we provide a separation example between QFL and low-rank tensor-based methods that highlights potential quantum query advantages. In simulation, QFL consistently outperforms strong classical baselines on small but diverse multimodal tasks, with particularly marked improvements in high-modality regimes. These results suggest that QFL offers a fundamentally new and scalable approach to multimodal fusion that merits deeper exploration on larger systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06938","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:57:58Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:57:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:57:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06888","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06888","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06888","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arkadeep","familyName":"Acharya","name":"Acharya, Arkadeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akash","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Akash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pradeepika","familyName":"Verma","name":"Verma, Pradeepika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kitsuchart","familyName":"Pasupa","name":"Pasupa, Kitsuchart","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sriparna","familyName":"Saha","name":"Saha, Sriparna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Priti","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Priti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"M3Retrieve: Benchmarking Multimodal Retrieval for Medicine"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T11:08:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:41:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the increasing use of RetrievalAugmented Generation (RAG), strong retrieval models have become more important than ever. In healthcare, multimodal retrieval models that combine information from both text and images offer major advantages for many downstream tasks such as question answering, cross-modal retrieval, and multimodal summarization, since medical data often includes both formats. However, there is currently no standard benchmark to evaluate how well these models perform in medical settings. To address this gap, we introduce M3Retrieve, a Multimodal Medical Retrieval Benchmark. M3Retrieve, spans 5 domains,16 medical fields, and 4 distinct tasks, with over 1.2 Million text documents and 164K multimodal queries, all collected under approved licenses. We evaluate leading multimodal retrieval models on this benchmark to explore the challenges specific to different medical specialities and to understand their impact on retrieval performance. By releasing M3Retrieve, we aim to enable systematic evaluation, foster model innovation, and accelerate research toward building more capable and reliable multimodal retrieval systems for medical applications. The dataset and the baselines code are available in this github page https://github.com/AkashGhosh/M3Retrieve."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP Mains 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06888","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:56:48Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:56:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:56:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06872","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06872","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06872","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frederic","familyName":"Gmeiner","name":"Gmeiner, Frederic","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenneth","familyName":"Holstein","name":"Holstein, Kenneth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolas","familyName":"Martelaro","name":"Martelaro, Nikolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prototyping Multimodal GenAI Real-Time Agents with Counterfactual Replays and Hybrid Wizard-of-Oz"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:39:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:40:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in multimodal generative AI (GenAI) enable the creation of personal context-aware real-time agents that, for example, can augment user workflows by following their on-screen activities and providing contextual assistance. However, prototyping such experiences is challenging, especially when supporting people with domain-specific tasks using real-time inputs such as speech and screen recordings. While prototyping an LLM-based proactive support agent system, we found that existing prototyping and evaluation methods were insufficient to anticipate the nuanced situational complexity and contextual immediacy required. To overcome these challenges, we explored a novel user-centered prototyping approach that combines counterfactual video replay prompting and hybrid Wizard-of-Oz methods to iteratively design and refine agent behaviors. This paper discusses our prototyping experiences, highlighting successes and limitations, and offers a practical guide and an open-source toolkit for UX designers, HCI researchers, and AI toolmakers to build more user-centered and context-aware multimodal agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 5 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06872","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:56:26Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:56:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:56:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06827","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06827","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06827","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaeseok","familyName":"Jeong","name":"Jeong, Jaeseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junho","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Junho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gayoung","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Gayoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunjey","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Yunjey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngjung","familyName":"Uh","name":"Uh, Youngjung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StyleKeeper: Prevent Content Leakage using Negative Visual Query Guidance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:50:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:37:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the domain of text-to-image generation, diffusion models have emerged as powerful tools. Recently, studies on visual prompting, where images are used as prompts, have enabled more precise control over style and content. However, existing methods often suffer from content leakage, where undesired elements of the visual style prompt are transferred along with the intended style. To address this issue, we 1) extend classifier-free guidance (CFG) to utilize swapping self-attention and propose 2) negative visual query guidance (NVQG) to reduce the transfer of unwanted contents. NVQG employs negative score by intentionally simulating content leakage scenarios that swap queries instead of key and values of self-attention layers from visual style prompts. This simple yet effective method significantly reduces content leakage. Furthermore, we provide careful solutions for using a real image as visual style prompts. Through extensive evaluation across various styles and text prompts, our method demonstrates superiority over existing approaches, reflecting the style of the references, and ensuring that resulting images match the text prompts. Our code is available \\href{https://github.com/naver-ai/StyleKeeper}{here}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICCV 2025; CVPRW AI4CC 2024 (Best Paper + Oral)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06827","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:22Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:55:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06782","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06782","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06782","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaichun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kaichun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GPT-5 Model Corrected GPT-4V's Chart Reading Errors, Not Prompting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:09:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:35:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a quantitative evaluation to understand the effect of zero-shot large-language model (LLMs) and prompting uses on chart reading tasks. We asked LLMs to answer 107 visualization questions to compare inference accuracies between the agentic GPT-5 and multimodal GPT-4V, for difficult image instances, where GPT-4V failed to produce correct answers. Our results show that model architecture dominates the inference accuracy: GPT5 largely improved accuracy, while prompt variants yielded only small effects. Pre-registration of this work is available here: https://osf.io/u78td/?view_only=6b075584311f48e991c39335c840ded3; the Google Drive materials are here:https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ll8WWZDf7cCNcfNWrLViWt8GwDNSvVrp/view."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06782","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:18Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:54:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06743","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06743","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06743","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria","familyName":"Levchenko","name":"Levchenko, Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating LLMs for Historical Document OCR: A Methodological Framework for Digital Humanities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68T50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T08:01:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:31:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Digital humanities scholars increasingly use Large Language Models for historical document digitization, yet lack appropriate evaluation frameworks for LLM-based OCR. Traditional metrics fail to capture temporal biases and period-specific errors crucial for historical corpus creation. We present an evaluation methodology for LLM-based historical OCR, addressing contamination risks and systematic biases in diplomatic transcription. Using 18th-century Russian Civil font texts, we introduce novel metrics including Historical Character Preservation Rate (HCPR) and Archaic Insertion Rate (AIR), alongside protocols for contamination control and stability testing. We evaluate 12 multimodal LLMs, finding that Gemini and Qwen models outperform traditional OCR while exhibiting over-historicization: inserting archaic characters from incorrect historical periods. Post-OCR correction degrades rather than improves performance. Our methodology provides digital humanities practitioners with guidelines for model selection and quality assessment in historical corpus digitization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The First Workshop on Natural Language Processing and Language Models for Digital Humanities (LM4DH 2025). RANLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06743","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:25Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:53:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06699","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06699","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06699","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gal","familyName":"Fadlon","name":"Fadlon, Gal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Idan","familyName":"Arbiv","name":"Arbiv, Idan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nimrod","familyName":"Berman","name":"Berman, Nimrod","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omri","familyName":"Azencot","name":"Azencot, Omri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Diffusion Model for Regular Time Series Generation from Irregular Data with Completion and Masking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T06:47:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:28:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generating realistic time series data is critical for applications in healthcare, finance, and science. However, irregular sampling and missing values present significant challenges. While prior methods address these irregularities, they often yield suboptimal results and incur high computational costs. Recent advances in regular time series generation, such as the diffusion-based ImagenTime model, demonstrate strong, fast, and scalable generative capabilities by transforming time series into image representations, making them a promising solution. However, extending ImagenTime to irregular sequences using simple masking introduces \"unnatural\" neighborhoods, where missing values replaced by zeros disrupt the learning process. To overcome this, we propose a novel two-step framework: first, a Time Series Transformer completes irregular sequences, creating natural neighborhoods; second, a vision-based diffusion model with masking minimizes dependence on the completed values. This approach leverages the strengths of both completion and masking, enabling robust and efficient generation of realistic time series. Our method achieves state-of-the-art performance, achieving a relative improvement in discriminative score by $70\\%$ and in computational cost by $85\\%$. Code is at https://github.com/azencot-group/ImagenI2R."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2025; The first two authors contributed equally and are co-leading authors"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06699","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:52:23Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:52:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:52:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06679","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06679","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06679","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohao","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Bohao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuechen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuechen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjia","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Junjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoru","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Haoru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sitong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Sitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengyao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chengyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yitong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinglong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xinglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bei","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Bei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaya","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Jiaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DreamOmni2: Multimodal Instruction-based Editing and Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T06:07:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:26:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in instruction-based image editing and subject-driven generation have garnered significant attention, yet both tasks still face limitations in meeting practical user needs. Instruction-based editing relies solely on language instructions, which often fail to capture specific editing details, making reference images necessary. Meanwhile, subject-driven generation is limited to combining concrete objects or people, overlooking broader, abstract concepts. To address these challenges, we propose two novel tasks: multimodal instruction-based editing and generation. These tasks support both text and image instructions and extend the scope to include both concrete and abstract concepts, greatly enhancing their practical applications. We introduce DreamOmni2, tackling two primary challenges: data creation and model framework design. Our data synthesis pipeline consists of three steps: (1) using a feature mixing method to create extraction data for both abstract and concrete concepts, (2) generating multimodal instruction-based editing training data using the editing and extraction models, and (3) further applying the extraction model to create training data for multimodal instruction-based editing. For the framework, to handle multi-image input, we propose an index encoding and position encoding shift scheme, which helps the model distinguish images and avoid pixel confusion. Additionally, we introduce joint training with the VLM and our generation/editing model to better process complex instructions. In addition, we have proposed comprehensive benchmarks for these two new tasks to drive their development. Experiments show that DreamOmni2 has achieved impressive results. Models and codes will be released."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06679","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:54Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:51:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06664","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06664","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06664","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunzhong","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yunzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangmin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yangmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hewei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunlong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yunlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zora Zhiruo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zora Zhiruo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ToolMem: Enhancing Multimodal Agents with Learnable Tool Capability Memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:32:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:25:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agents utilizing tools powered by large language models (LLMs) or vision-language models (VLMs) have demonstrated remarkable progress in diverse tasks across text and visual modalities. Unlike traditional tools such as calculators, which give deterministic outputs, neural tools perform uncertainly across task scenarios. While different tools for a task may excel in varied scenarios, existing agents typically rely on fixed tools, thus limiting the flexibility in selecting the most suitable tool for specific tasks. In contrast, humans snowball their understanding of the capabilities of different tools by interacting with them, and apply this knowledge to select the optimal tool when solving a future task. To build agents that similarly benefit from this process, we propose ToolMem that enables agents to develop memories of tool capabilities from previous interactions, by summarizing their strengths and weaknesses and storing them in memory; at inference, the agent can retrieve relevant entries from ToolMem, and select the best tool to solve individual tasks more accurately. We evaluate ToolMem on learning varied text generation and text-to-image generation neural tools. Compared to no-memory, generic agents, we find ToolMem-augmented agents predict tool performance 14.8% and 28.7% more accurately across text and multimodal generation scenarios. Moreover, ToolMem facilitates optimal tool selection among multiple choices by 21% and 24% absolute increases in respective scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06664","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:33Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:51:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06657","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06657","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06657","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyuan","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Boyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yueqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiloni","familyName":"Mehta","name":"Mehta, Hiloni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mick","familyName":"Zomnir","name":"Zomnir, Mick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omkar","familyName":"Pathak","name":"Pathak, Omkar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changping","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Changping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruolin","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Ruolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yajun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Yajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dapeng","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Dapeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xia","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Mingyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Onkar","familyName":"Dalal","name":"Dalal, Onkar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningren","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Ningren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Powered Nuanced Video Attribute Annotation for Enhanced Recommendations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:17:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:25:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3705328.3748103","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a case study on deploying Large Language Models (LLMs) as an advanced \"annotation\" mechanism to achieve nuanced content understanding (e.g., discerning content \"vibe\") at scale within a large-scale industrial short-form video recommendation system. Traditional machine learning classifiers for content understanding face protracted development cycles and a lack of deep, nuanced comprehension. The \"LLM-as-annotators\" approach addresses these by significantly shortening development times and enabling the annotation of subtle attributes. This work details an end-to-end workflow encompassing: (1) iterative definition and robust evaluation of target attributes, refined by offline metrics and online A/B testing; (2) scalable offline bulk annotation of video corpora using LLMs with multimodal features, optimized inference, and knowledge distillation for broad application; and (3) integration of these rich annotations into the online recommendation serving system, for example, through personalized restrict retrieval. Experimental results demonstrate the efficacy of this approach, with LLMs outperforming human raters in offline annotation quality for nuanced attributes and yielding significant improvements of user participation and satisfied consumption in online A/B tests. The study provides insights into designing and scaling production-level LLM pipelines for rich content evaluation, highlighting the adaptability and benefits of LLM-generated nuanced understanding for enhancing content discovery, user satisfaction, and the overall effectiveness of modern recommendation systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"RecSys 2025 Industry Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06657","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:24Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:51:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06633","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06633","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06633","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kruthika","familyName":"Gangaraju","name":"Gangaraju, Kruthika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanmayi","familyName":"Inaparthy","name":"Inaparthy, Tanmayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengpei","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Fengpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Assist-As-Needed: Adaptive Multimodal Robotic Assistance for Medication Management in Dementia Care"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T04:34:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:23:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"People living with dementia (PLWDs) face progressively declining abilities in medication management-from simple forgetfulness to complete task breakdown-yet most assistive technologies fail to adapt to these changing needs. This one-size-fits-all approach undermines autonomy, accelerates dependence, and increases caregiver burden. Occupational therapy principles emphasize matching assistance levels to individual capabilities: minimal reminders for those who merely forget, spatial guidance for those who misplace items, and comprehensive multimodal support for those requiring step-by-step instruction. However, existing robotic systems lack this adaptive, graduated response framework essential for maintaining PLWD independence. We present an adaptive multimodal robotic framework using the Pepper robot that dynamically adjusts assistance based on real-time assessment of user needs. Our system implements a hierarchical intervention model progressing from (1) simple verbal reminders, to (2) verbal + gestural cues, to (3) full multimodal guidance combining physical navigation to medication locations with step-by-step verbal and gestural instructions. Powered by LLM-driven interaction strategies and multimodal sensing, the system continuously evaluates task states to provide just-enough assistance-preserving autonomy while ensuring medication adherence. We conducted a preliminary study with healthy adults and dementia care stakeholders in a controlled lab setting, evaluating the system's usability, comprehensibility, and appropriateness of adaptive feedback mechanisms. This work contributes: (1) a theoretically grounded adaptive assistance framework translating occupational therapy principles into HRI design, (2) a multimodal robotic implementation that preserves PLWD dignity through graduated support, and (3) empirical insights into stakeholder perceptions of adaptive robotic care."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06633","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:50:50Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:50:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:50:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06590","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06590","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06590","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Ziyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"DanDan","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, DanDan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaixiang","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Kaixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weilong","familyName":"Chai","name":"Chai, Weilong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxin","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jianxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Libin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Libin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongjie","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Yongjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taozhi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Taozhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajia","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiajia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingpei","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qingpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingdong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jingdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ming-UniVision: Joint Image Understanding and Generation with a Unified Continuous Tokenizer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T02:50:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:19:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual tokenization remains a core challenge in unifying visual understanding and generation within the autoregressive paradigm. Existing methods typically employ tokenizers in discrete latent spaces to align with the tokens from large language models, where the quantization errors can limit semantic expressiveness and degrade the capability of vision-language understanding. To address this, we introduce MingTok, a new family of visual tokenizers with a continuous latent space, for unified autoregressive generation and understanding. While understanding tasks favor discriminative high-dimensional features, generation tasks prefer compact low-level codes. Thus, to reconcile these competing demands, MingTok adopts a three-stage sequential architecture involving low-level encoding, semantic expansion, and visual reconstruction. Built on top of it, Ming-UniVision eliminates the need for task-specific visual representations, and unifies diverse vision-language tasks under a single autoregrsssive prediction paradigm. By formulating both understanding and generation as next-token prediction in a shared continuous space, it seamlessly supports multi-round, in-context tasks such as iterative understanding, generation and editing. Empirically, we find that using a unified continuous visual representation reconciles the competing requirements on the tokenizers by the understanding and generation tasks, thereby leading to state-of-the-art level performance across both domains. We hope our findings will facilitate unified visual tokenization in the continuous domain. Inference code and model weights are released to benefit community."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code released at https://github.com/inclusionAI/Ming-UniVision"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06590","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:49:48Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:49:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:49:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06529","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06529","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06529","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiangyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Théophane","familyName":"Vallaeys","name":"Vallaeys, Théophane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maha","familyName":"Elbayad","name":"Elbayad, Maha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakob","familyName":"Verbeek","name":"Verbeek, Jakob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VUGEN: Visual Understanding priors for GENeration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T00:04:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:13:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have enabled unified understanding across text and images, yet equipping these models with robust image generation capabilities remains challenging. Existing approaches often rely on reconstruction-oriented autoencoders or complex bridging mechanisms, leading to misalignment between understanding and generation representations, or architectural complexity. In this work, we propose VUGEN, a novel framework that explicitly leverages VLM's pretrained visual understanding priors for efficient and high-quality image generation. Our approach first transforms the high-dimensional latent space of the VLM's native vision encoder into a lower-dimensional, tractable distribution that maximally preserves visual information. The VLM is then trained to sample within this reduced latent space, ensuring alignment with its visual understanding capabilities. Finally, a dedicated pixel decoder maps these generated latents back to the image space. We find that a VAE-free pixel diffusion decoder to be on par or better than commonly used complex latent diffusion decoders that internally rely on VAE latents. Extensive experiments demonstrate that VUGEN achieves superior image generation performance, improving DPG Bench from 71.17 to 74.32 and FID from 11.86 to 9.06 on COCO, while fully preserving the VLM's original understanding capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06529","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:48:24Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:48:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:48:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06512","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06512","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06512","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avishree","familyName":"Khare","name":"Khare, Avishree","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hideki","familyName":"Okamoto","name":"Okamoto, Hideki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bardh","familyName":"Hoxha","name":"Hoxha, Bardh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgios","familyName":"Fainekos","name":"Fainekos, Georgios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajeev","familyName":"Alur","name":"Alur, Rajeev","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LogSTOP: Temporal Scores over Prediction Sequences for Matching and Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T23:05:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:12:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural models such as YOLO and HuBERT can be used to detect local properties such as objects (\"car\") and emotions (\"angry\") in individual frames of videos and audio clips respectively. The likelihood of these detections is indicated by scores in [0, 1]. Lifting these scores to temporal properties over sequences can be useful for several downstream applications such as query matching (e.g., \"does the speaker eventually sound happy in this audio clip?\"), and ranked retrieval (e.g., \"retrieve top 5 videos with a 10 second scene where a car is detected until a pedestrian is detected\"). In this work, we formalize this problem of assigning Scores for TempOral Properties (STOPs) over sequences, given potentially noisy score predictors for local properties. We then propose a scoring function called LogSTOP that can efficiently compute these scores for temporal properties represented in Linear Temporal Logic. Empirically, LogSTOP, with YOLO and HuBERT, outperforms Large Vision / Audio Language Models and other Temporal Logic-based baselines by at least 16% on query matching with temporal properties over objects-in-videos and emotions-in-speech respectively. Similarly, on ranked retrieval with temporal properties over objects and actions in videos, LogSTOP with Grounding DINO and SlowR50 reports at least a 19% and 16% increase in mean average precision and recall over zero-shot text-to-video retrieval baselines respectively."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06512","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:48:00Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:48:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:48:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06448","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06448","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06448","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prabhant","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Prabhant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sibylle","familyName":"Hess","name":"Hess, Sibylle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joaquin","familyName":"Vanschoren","name":"Vanschoren, Joaquin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How NOT to benchmark your SITE metric: Beyond Static Leaderboards and Towards Realistic Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T20:38:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:08:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transferability estimation metrics are used to find a high-performing pre-trained model for a given target task without fine-tuning models and without access to the source dataset. Despite the growing interest in developing such metrics, the benchmarks used to measure their progress have gone largely unexamined. In this work, we empirically show the shortcomings of widely used benchmark setups to evaluate transferability estimation metrics. We argue that the benchmarks on which these metrics are evaluated are fundamentally flawed. We empirically demonstrate that their unrealistic model spaces and static performance hierarchies artificially inflate the perceived performance of existing metrics, to the point where simple, dataset-agnostic heuristics can outperform sophisticated methods. Our analysis reveals a critical disconnect between current evaluation protocols and the complexities of real-world model selection. To address this, we provide concrete recommendations for constructing more robust and realistic benchmarks to guide future research in a more meaningful direction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06448","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:46:31Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:46:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:46:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06386","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06386","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06386","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Van de Cruys","name":"Van de Cruys, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Controllable Stylistic Text Generation with Train-Time Attribute-Regularized Diffusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T19:09:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:04:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generating stylistic text with specific attributes is a key problem in controllable text generation. Recently, diffusion models have emerged as a powerful paradigm for both visual and textual generation. Existing approaches can be broadly categorized into classifier-free guidance (CFG) and classifier guidance (CG) methods. While CFG effectively preserves semantic content, it often fails to provide effective attribute control. In contrast, CG modifies the denoising trajectory using classifier gradients, enabling better attribute alignment but incurring high computational costs during sampling and suffering from classifier generalization issues. In this work, we propose RegDiff, a regularized diffusion framework that leverages attribute features without requiring a pretrained classifier during sampling, thereby achieving controllable generation with reduced computational costs. Specifically, RegDiff employs a VAE-based encoder--decoder architecture to ensure reconstruction fidelity and a latent diffusion model trained with attribute supervision to enable controllable text generation. Attribute information is injected only during training. Experiments on five datasets spanning multiple stylistic attributes demonstrate that RegDiff outperforms strong baselines in generating stylistic texts. These results validate the effectiveness of RegDiff as an efficient solution for attribute-controllable text diffusion. Our code, datasets, and resources will be released upon publication at https://github.com/xxxx."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06386","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:45:06Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:45:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:45:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06335","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06335","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06335","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed","familyName":"Alsubaie","name":"Alsubaie, Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wenxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linxia","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Linxia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ovidiu C.","familyName":"Andronesi","name":"Andronesi, Ovidiu C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sirani M.","familyName":"Perera","name":"Perera, Sirani M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xianqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Conditional Denoising Diffusion Model-Based Robust MR Image Reconstruction from Highly Undersampled Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:01:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:02:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is a critical tool in modern medical diagnostics, yet its prolonged acquisition time remains a critical limitation, especially in time-sensitive clinical scenarios. While undersampling strategies can accelerate image acquisition, they often result in image artifacts and degraded quality. Recent diffusion models have shown promise for reconstructing high-fidelity images from undersampled data by learning powerful image priors; however, most existing approaches either (i) rely on unsupervised score functions without paired supervision or (ii) apply data consistency only as a post-processing step. In this work, we introduce a conditional denoising diffusion framework with iterative data-consistency correction, which differs from prior methods by embedding the measurement model directly into every reverse diffusion step and training the model on paired undersampled-ground truth data. This hybrid design bridges generative flexibility with explicit enforcement of MRI physics. Experiments on the fastMRI dataset demonstrate that our framework consistently outperforms recent state-of-the-art deep learning and diffusion-based methods in SSIM, PSNR, and LPIPS, with LPIPS capturing perceptual improvements more faithfully. These results demonstrate that integrating conditional supervision with iterative consistency updates yields substantial improvements in both pixel-level fidelity and perceptual realism, establishing a principled and practical advance toward robust, accelerated MRI reconstruction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06335","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:43:54Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:43:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:43:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06308","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06308","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06308","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Xin","name":"Xin, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siqi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Siqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiwen","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Kaiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juncheng","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Juncheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Tai","name":"Tai, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuewen","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yuewen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Keqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yibin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinbin","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Jinbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dengyang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Dengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuandong","familyName":"Pu","name":"Pu, Yuandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haoxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Le","familyName":"Zhuo","name":"Zhuo, Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjun","familyName":"He","name":"He, Junjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gen","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Gen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianbin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenglong","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Shenglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongsheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangtao","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Guangtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianfan","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Tianfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaohong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lumina-DiMOO: An Omni Diffusion Large Language Model for Multi-Modal Generation and Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:59:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:02:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Lumina-DiMOO, an open-source foundational model for seamless multi-modal generation and understanding. Lumina-DiMOO sets itself apart from prior unified models by utilizing a fully discrete diffusion modeling to handle inputs and outputs across various modalities. This innovative approach allows Lumina-DiMOO to achieve higher sampling efficiency compared to previous autoregressive (AR) or hybrid AR-Diffusion paradigms and adeptly support a broad spectrum of multi-modal tasks, including text-to-image generation, image-to-image generation (e.g., image editing, subject-driven generation, and image inpainting, etc.), as well as image understanding. Lumina-DiMOO achieves state-of-the-art performance on multiple benchmarks, surpassing existing open-source unified multi-modal models. To foster further advancements in multi-modal and discrete diffusion model research, we release our code and checkpoints to the community. Project Page: https://synbol.github.io/Lumina-DiMOO."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 13 figures, 10 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06308","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:43:16Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:43:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:43:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06295","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06295","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06295","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Young D.","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Young D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhinav","familyName":"Mehrotra","name":"Mehrotra, Abhinav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Malcolm","familyName":"Chadwick","name":"Chadwick, Malcolm","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto Gil","familyName":"Ramos","name":"Ramos, Alberto Gil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sourav","familyName":"Bhattacharya","name":"Bhattacharya, Sourav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient High-Resolution Image Editing with Hallucination-Aware Loss and Adaptive Tiling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:09:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-resolution (4K) image-to-image synthesis has become increasingly important for mobile applications. Existing diffusion models for image editing face significant challenges, in terms of memory and image quality, when deployed on resource-constrained devices. In this paper, we present MobilePicasso, a novel system that enables efficient image editing at high resolutions, while minimising computational cost and memory usage. MobilePicasso comprises three stages: (i) performing image editing at a standard resolution with hallucination-aware loss, (ii) applying latent projection to overcome going to the pixel space, and (iii) upscaling the edited image latent to a higher resolution with adaptive context-preserving tiling. Our user study with 46 participants reveals that MobilePicasso not only improves image quality by 18-48% but reduces hallucinations by 14-51% over existing methods. MobilePicasso demonstrates significantly lower latency, e.g., up to 55.8$\\times$ speed-up, yet with a small increase in runtime memory, e.g., a mere 9% increase over prior work. Surprisingly, the on-device runtime of MobilePicasso is observed to be faster than a server-based high-resolution image editing model running on an A100 GPU."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint. Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06295","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:57Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:42:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06291","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06291","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06291","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningcong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ningcong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanhua","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yanhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shen","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Shen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Traj-Transformer: Diffusion Models with Transformer for GPS Trajectory Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T05:41:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The widespread use of GPS devices has driven advances in spatiotemporal data mining, enabling machine learning models to simulate human decision making and generate realistic trajectories, addressing both data collection costs and privacy concerns. Recent studies have shown the promise of diffusion models for high-quality trajectory generation. However, most existing methods rely on convolution based architectures (e.g. UNet) to predict noise during the diffusion process, which often results in notable deviations and the loss of fine-grained street-level details due to limited model capacity. In this paper, we propose Trajectory Transformer, a novel model that employs a transformer backbone for both conditional information embedding and noise prediction. We explore two GPS coordinate embedding strategies, location embedding and longitude-latitude embedding, and analyze model performance at different scales. Experiments on two real-world datasets demonstrate that Trajectory Transformer significantly enhances generation quality and effectively alleviates the deviation issues observed in prior approaches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06291","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:52Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:42:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06283","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06283","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06283","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sashank","familyName":"Makanaboyina","name":"Makanaboyina, Sashank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SER-Diff: Synthetic Error Replay Diffusion for Incremental Brain Tumor Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T21:53:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Incremental brain tumor segmentation is critical for models that must adapt to evolving clinical datasets without retraining on all prior data. However, catastrophic forgetting, where models lose previously acquired knowledge, remains a major obstacle. Recent incremental learning frameworks with knowledge distillation partially mitigate forgetting but rely heavily on generative replay or auxiliary storage. Meanwhile, diffusion models have proven effective for refining tumor segmentations, but have not been explored in incremental learning contexts. We propose Synthetic Error Replay Diffusion (SER-Diff), the first framework that unifies diffusion-based refinement with incremental learning. SER-Diff leverages a frozen teacher diffusion model to generate synthetic error maps from past tasks, which are replayed during training on new tasks. A dual-loss formulation combining Dice loss for new data and knowledge distillation loss for replayed errors ensures both adaptability and retention. Experiments on BraTS2020, BraTS2021, and BraTS2023 demonstrate that SER-Diff consistently outperforms prior methods. It achieves the highest Dice scores of 95.8\\%, 94.9\\%, and 94.6\\%, along with the lowest HD95 values of 4.4 mm, 4.7 mm, and 4.9 mm, respectively. These results indicate that SER-Diff not only mitigates catastrophic forgetting but also delivers more accurate and anatomically coherent segmentations across evolving datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06283","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:40Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:42:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06280","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06280","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06280","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zohaib Hasan","familyName":"Siddiqui","name":"Siddiqui, Zohaib Hasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dayam","familyName":"Nadeem","name":"Nadeem, Dayam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Masudur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Mohammad Masudur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Nadeem","name":"Nadeem, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shahab Saquib","familyName":"Sohail","name":"Sohail, Shahab Saquib","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beenish Moalla","familyName":"Chaudhry","name":"Chaudhry, Beenish Moalla","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Surgeons Are Indian Males and Speech Therapists Are White Females: Auditing Biases in Vision-Language Models for Healthcare Professionals"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T20:20:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision language models (VLMs), such as CLIP and OpenCLIP, can encode and reflect stereotypical associations between medical professions and demographic attributes learned from web-scale data. We present an evaluation protocol for healthcare settings that quantifies associated biases and assesses their operational risk. Our methodology (i) defines a taxonomy spanning clinicians and allied healthcare roles (e.g., surgeon, cardiologist, dentist, nurse, pharmacist, technician), (ii) curates a profession-aware prompt suite to probe model behavior, and (iii) benchmarks demographic skew against a balanced face corpus. Empirically, we observe consistent demographic biases across multiple roles and vision models. Our work highlights the importance of bias identification in critical domains such as healthcare as AI-enabled hiring and workforce analytics can have downstream implications for equity, compliance, and patient trust."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06280","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:36Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:42:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06276","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06276","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06276","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehdi","familyName":"Rabiee","name":"Rabiee, Mehdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergio","familyName":"Greco","name":"Greco, Sergio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reza","familyName":"Shahbazian","name":"Shahbazian, Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irina","familyName":"Trubitsyna","name":"Trubitsyna, Irina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Total Variation Regularized Framework for Epilepsy-Related MRI Image Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:07:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Focal Cortical Dysplasia (FCD) is a primary cause of drug-resistant epilepsy and is difficult to detect in brain {magnetic resonance imaging} (MRI) due to the subtle and small-scale nature of its lesions. Accurate segmentation of FCD regions in 3D multimodal brain MRI images is essential for effective surgical planning and treatment. However, this task remains highly challenging due to the limited availability of annotated FCD datasets, the extremely small size and weak contrast of FCD lesions, the complexity of handling 3D multimodal inputs, and the need for output smoothness and anatomical consistency, which is often not addressed by standard voxel-wise loss functions. This paper presents a new framework for segmenting FCD regions in 3D brain MRI images. We adopt state-of-the-art transformer-enhanced encoder-decoder architecture and introduce a novel loss function combining Dice loss with an anisotropic {Total Variation} (TV) term. This integration encourages spatial smoothness and reduces false positive clusters without relying on post-processing. The framework is evaluated on a public FCD dataset with 85 epilepsy patients and demonstrates superior segmentation accuracy and consistency compared to standard loss formulations. The model with the proposed TV loss shows an 11.9\\% improvement on the Dice coefficient and 13.3\\% higher precision over the baseline model. Moreover, the number of false positive clusters is reduced by 61.6%"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06276","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:31Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:42:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06267","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06267","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06267","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khartik","familyName":"Uppalapati","name":"Uppalapati, Khartik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shakeel","familyName":"Abdulkareem","name":"Abdulkareem, Shakeel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bora","familyName":"Yimenicioglu","name":"Yimenicioglu, Bora","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RareGraph-Synth: Knowledge-Guided Diffusion Models for Generating Privacy-Preserving Synthetic Patient Trajectories in Ultra-Rare Diseases"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; H.2.8; J.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T03:59:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose RareGraph-Synth, a knowledge-guided, continuous-time diffusion framework that generates realistic yet privacy-preserving synthetic electronic-health-record (EHR) trajectories for ultra-rare diseases. RareGraph-Synth unifies five public resources: Orphanet/Orphadata, the Human Phenotype Ontology (HPO), the GARD rare-disease KG, PrimeKG, and the FDA Adverse Event Reporting System (FAERS) into a heterogeneous knowledge graph comprising approximately 8 M typed edges. Meta-path scores extracted from this 8-million-edge KG modulate the per-token noise schedule in the forward stochastic differential equation, steering generation toward biologically plausible lab-medication-adverse-event co-occurrences while retaining score-based diffusion model stability. The reverse denoiser then produces timestamped sequences of lab-code, medication-code, and adverse-event-flag triples that contain no protected health information. On simulated ultra-rare-disease cohorts, RareGraph-Synth lowers categorical Maximum Mean Discrepancy by 40 percent relative to an unguided diffusion baseline and by greater than 60 percent versus GAN counterparts, without sacrificing downstream predictive utility. A black-box membership-inference evaluation using the DOMIAS attacker yields AUROC approximately 0.53, well below the 0.55 safe-release threshold and substantially better than the approximately 0.61 plus or minus 0.03 observed for non-KG baselines, demonstrating strong resistance to re-identification. These results suggest that integrating biomedical knowledge graphs directly into diffusion noise schedules can simultaneously enhance fidelity and privacy, enabling safer data sharing for rare-disease research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 2 figures, 2 tables. Submitted to IEEE International Conference on Data Science and Advanced Analytics (DSAA)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06267","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:18Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:42:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06252","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06252","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06252","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yann","familyName":"Bellec","name":"Bellec, Yann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dream2Image : An Open Multimodal EEG Dataset for Decoding and Visualizing Dreams with Artificial Intelligence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T22:43:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Dream2Image is the world's first dataset combining EEG signals, dream transcriptions, and AI-generated images. Based on 38 participants and more than 31 hours of dream EEG recordings, it contains 129 samples offering: the final seconds of brain activity preceding awakening (T-15, T-30, T-60, T-120), raw reports of dream experiences, and an approximate visual reconstruction of the dream. This dataset provides a novel resource for dream research, a unique resource to study the neural correlates of dreaming, to develop models for decoding dreams from brain activity, and to explore new approaches in neuroscience, psychology, and artificial intelligence. Available in open access on Hugging Face and GitHub, Dream2Image provides a multimodal resource designed to support research at the interface of artificial intelligence and neuroscience. It was designed to inspire researchers and extend the current approaches to brain activity decoding. Limitations include the relatively small sample size and the variability of dream recall, which may affect generalizability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 Pages, 3 Figures, The Dream2Image dataset is openly available on Hugging Face at: https://huggingface.co/datasets/opsecsystems/Dream2Image"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06252","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:58Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:41:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06235","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06235","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06235","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Scholz","name":"Scholz, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunal","familyName":"Bagga","name":"Bagga, Kunal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christine","familyName":"Ahrends","name":"Ahrends, Christine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlo Alberto","familyName":"Barbano","name":"Barbano, Carlo Alberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stacked Regression using Off-the-shelf, Stimulus-tuned and Fine-tuned Neural Networks for Predicting fMRI Brain Responses to Movies (Algonauts 2025 Report)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T15:24:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present our submission to the Algonauts 2025 Challenge, where the goal is to predict fMRI brain responses to movie stimuli. Our approach integrates multimodal representations from large language models, video encoders, audio models, and vision-language models, combining both off-the-shelf and fine-tuned variants. To improve performance, we enhanced textual inputs with detailed transcripts and summaries, and we explored stimulus-tuning and fine-tuning strategies for language and vision models. Predictions from individual models were combined using stacked regression, yielding solid results. Our submission, under the team name Seinfeld, ranked 10th. We make all code and resources publicly available, contributing to ongoing efforts in developing multimodal encoding models for brain activity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06235","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:33Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:41:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":78,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-08T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-09T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28multimodal+OR+%22multi-modal%22+OR+%22vision+language%22+OR+%22vision-language%22+OR+VLA+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22diffusion+model%22+OR+%22diffusion+models%22+OR+%22generative+model%22+OR+%22generative+models%22%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered"}}